The `ToUpper` method in .NET's `System.Globalization` namespace does not require more memory when the `destination` span has the same length as the `source` span. This is because the method allocates a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be smaller than the destination span if the source span is longer.

However, there are some edge cases to consider:

- If the source span is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.
- If the culture is null, the method will throw a NullReferenceException.
- If the source span is shorter than the destination span, the method will allocate a new span of the same length as the source span, which can be larger than the destination span.

To avoid these edge cases, you should always check for null values and handle them accordingly.

```csharp
public static string ToUpper(this ReadOnlySpan<char> source, Span<char> destination, CultureInfo culture)
{
    if (source == null || destination == null)
    {
        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(source), "Source and destination spans cannot be null");
    }

    if (source.Length > destination.Length)
    {
        destination = stackalloc char[source.Length];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < source.Length; i++)
    {
        destination[i] = char.ToUpper(source[i]);
    }

    return new string(destination);
}
```

Questions:
Title: C/C++ warn or prohibit literal string concatenation.
Body: <p>Is there a way to warn or prohibit literal string concatenations such as:</p>
<pre><code>const char *a = &quot;foo&quot; &quot; bar&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>I spent hours finding a bug in a big static array that had</p>
<pre><code>const char * a[] = {&quot;foo&quot; &quot;bar&quot;};
</code></pre>
<p>instead of</p>
<pre><code>const char * a[] = {&quot;foo&quot;, &quot;bar&quot;};
</code></pre>

Answers:
None
You can use the `std::string` class in C++ to avoid literal string concatenation. Instead of concatenating strings using the `+` operator, you can use the `std::string` constructor to build the string.

For example:

```cpp
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string str = "foo";
    str += " bar";
    std::cout << str << std::endl;  // Outputs: foo bar
    return 0;
}
```

This approach is more readable and safer than concatenating strings using the `+` operator.

None

The C++ code provided is not well-formed because it does not declare the function pointer `my_function` before using it. In C++, function pointers must be declared before they are used. The correct code should be:

```cpp
void my_function(const char*);

void my_function(const char* ptr) {
    std::string s(ptr);
    // ...
}
```

The `std::string` object will not be destroyed until the `my_function` function returns, at which point it will be automatically deleted. Therefore, using a `std::string` object in a function pointer is not a good practice.

```csharp
string text = """
    This is a multi-line string in C#.
    It uses triple double quotes for multiline text.
    This is a second line of text.
    And another line.
    The triple double quotes are not necessary
    in this case because the text is not
    enclosed in single double quotes.
    """

public class TripleDoubleQuotesExample
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(text);
    }
}
```

