Title: thread_local + std::thread deadlock on destruction.
Body: <p>Anyone knows what kind of UB is this? The following code deadlocks on <code>jthread</code> destruction when built with MSVC 19.29.30148, sometimes it deadlocks after std::cout and sometimes before.
This is somehow related to thread_local, but I cannot see what is the issue. It seems to work fine under other compilers and platforms.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;thread&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main(void)
{
    std::thread t2(
        [&amp;] {
            thread_local std::jthread asd([] {
                    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;ASD&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
                    });
        });
    if (t2.joinable()) { t2.join(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Update: Reproducible both on static and dynamic runtime</p>

Answers:
None
The issue lies in the way you're using `std::thread_local`. When a thread-local object is destroyed, its memory is freed, and the `std::shared_ptr` becomes invalid. However, the `std::thread_local` storage is not automatically managed by the `std::shared_ptr`. It's a separate storage that's managed by the thread itself. When the thread is destroyed, the `std::thread_local` storage is freed, which can lead to undefined behavior.

In your case, the `std::thread_local` storage is being accessed by multiple threads, and when one thread is destroyed, the storage is freed, which can cause the other threads to access memory that's already been freed. This leads to undefined behavior, including the SIGSEGV you're seeing.

To fix this issue, you need to ensure that the `std::thread_local` storage is properly managed by the `std::shared_ptr`. One way to do this is to use `std::shared_ptr<std::thread_local>` instead of `std::thread_local std::shared_ptr`. This will ensure that the `std::thread_local` storage is properly managed by the `std::shared_ptr`.

Here's an updated version of your code:
```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <thread>

void f() {
    std::shared_ptr<std::thread_local> ptr = std::make_shared<std::thread_local>([] { return 0; });
}

int main() {
    std::thread th(f);
    th.join();
    return 0;
}
```
Alternatively, you can use a `std::atomic` variable to manage the `std::thread_local` storage, which will ensure that the storage is properly managed by the `std::shared_ptr` even when the thread is destroyed:
```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <thread>

std::atomic<int> thread_local_var(0);

void f() {
    std::shared_ptr<std::thread_local> ptr = std::make_shared<std::thread_local>(&thread_local_var);
}

int main() {
    std::thread th(f);
    th.join();
    return 0;
}
```
Both of these approaches should fix the issue and prevent the SIGSEGV you're seeing.

