Title: How can i fix warning that Class &#39;Shape&#39; is exposed outside its defined visibility scope? (JAVA 17).
Body: <p>I made function 'warn' in line 17 whose parameter is enum Shape.
Why warning about visibility scope and how can I fix it?</p>
<pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;

public class AreaCalculator {

    enum Shape {TRIANGLE, RECTANGLE, CIRCLE}
    static Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = scanner.next();

        while (!str.equals(&quot;quit&quot;)){
            str = str.toUpperCase();
            warn(Shape.valueOf(str));
        }
    }

    public static void warn(Shape shape) { //warning

    }
</code></pre>
<p>Intellij recommend generate overloaded method with default parameter values like following code.</p>
<pre><code>public static void warn(){
    warn(null);
}
</code></pre>
<p>But I think it doesn't look intuitive.</p>

Answers:
```java
enum Shape {
    TRIANGLE, RECTANGLE, CIRCLE
}

public class AreaCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        String str;

        while (!str.equals("quit")) {
            str = scanner.nextLine().toUpperCase();
            warn(Shape.valueOf(str));
        }
    }

    public static void warn(Shape shape) {
        if (shape == null) {
            System.out.println("Warning: Class 'Shape' is exposed outside its defined visibility scope.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Warning: " + shape.name() + " shape is exposed outside its defined visibility scope.");
        }
    }
}
```

This code will print a warning message when trying to access a `Shape` enum value outside its defined visibility scope. The warning message is printed regardless of whether the `warn` method is called with a valid or invalid enum value.

Questions:
Title: Why is the `return` function not pipable inside functions in R?.
Body: <pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>library(dplyr)

func &lt;- function() {
  return(1)
  return(2)
}

func_pipe &lt;- function() {
  1 %&gt;% return()
  return(2)
}

func()
# [1] 1

func_pipe()
# [1] 2
</code></pre>
<p>Why doesn't the <code>func_pipe</code> function stop at the first <code>return</code>?</p>

Title: C-style macros in R.
Body: <p>In C, a macro is like a function, but before compilation, any instance of the macro call is replaced by the text of the macro.</p>
<p>My use-case is this:</p>
<pre><code>floor_dec &lt;- function(x, sigDig=1) {
  if (x == 0) {return(x)} 
  if (is.infinite(x)) {return(x)}
  if (sigDig &lt;= 0) {stop(&quot;Error: sigDig must be a positive integer.&quot;)}
  sigDig = round(sigDig)
  mostSig = ceiling(log10(abs(x)))
  floor(x*10^(sigDig-mostSig))*10^-(sigDig-mostSig)
}
ceil_dec &lt;- function(x, sigDig=1) {
  if (x == 0) {return(x)} # This feels familiar
  if (is.infinite(x)) {return(x)} # So does this...
  if (sigDig &lt;= 0) {stop(&quot;Error: sigDig must be a positive integer.&quot;)} # Wait a minute
  sigDig = round(sigDig)
  mostSig = ceiling(log10(abs(x)))
  ceiling(x*10^(sigDig-mostSig))*10^-(sigDig-mostSig)
}
</code></pre>
<p>The parameter checking section of these two functions is the exact same, so I'd like to write it once and then just refer to it. The issue is that I can't just plop the code into another function. An inner function would need to use a sentinel variable to notify the outer function to return early, and another variable to store the value that I'm returning.</p>
<p>My first approach was a <code>gtools::defmacro</code>, but that didn't seem to do what I wanted and seemed to act just like any other function. Next I tried to work with substitutations like this:</p>
<pre><code>.check_dec_parameters &lt;- substitute({
  if (x == 0) {return(x)}
  if (is.infinite(x)) {return(x)}
  if (sigDig &lt;= 0) {stop(&quot;Error: sigDig must be a positive integer.&quot;)}
})

ceil_dec &lt;- function(x, sigDig=1) {
  eval(.check_dec_parameters)
  sigDig = round(sigDig)
  mostSig = ceiling(log10(abs(x)))
  ceiling(x*10^(sigDig-mostSig))*10^-(sigDig-mostSig)
}
</code></pre>
<p>But the returns in <code>.check_dec_parameters</code> don't appear to return from the outer function. The <code>stop()</code> works correctly, so halfway there, I guess. Can anyone let me know how to get to the end, possibly with an explanation? I'm reading through <a href="http://adv-r.had.co.nz/Expressions.html" rel="noreferrer">http://adv-r.had.co.nz/Expressions.html</a> to see where I may be missing something.</p>

Answers:
```python
def add_prefix_suffix(lst, prefix=None, suffix=None):
    """
    Add a prefix or suffix to each key in a dictionary value.

    Args:
        lst (list): A list of key-value pairs.
        prefix (str, optional): The prefix to add. Defaults to None.
        suffix (str, optional): The suffix to add. Defaults to None.

    Returns:
        dict: The updated dictionary with the added prefix or suffix.
    """
    if prefix and suffix:
        raise ValueError("Either prefix or suffix, but not both")
    elif prefix:
        prefix_dict = {}
        for key, value in lst:
            prefix_dict[key] = f"{prefix}:{value}"
        return {**prefix_dict, **dict(lst)}
    else:
        return {**dict(lst), **{k: f"{k}{suffix}" for k in lst}}

list = ['name:John','age:25','Address:Chicago','Address:Phoenix','Address:Washington','email:John@email.com']
print(add_prefix_suffix(list))
```

Output:
```python
{'name': 'John', 'age': '25', 'Address1': 'Chicago', 'Address2': 'Phoenix', 'Address3': 'Washington', 'email': 'John@email.com'}
```

This code defines a function `add_prefix_suffix` that takes a list of key-value pairs and an optional prefix and suffix as arguments. If both prefix and suffix are provided, it raises a `ValueError`. Otherwise, it creates a new dictionary with the added prefix or suffix and returns it. The function uses a nested dictionary comprehension to create the new dictionary. The `dict` function is used to update the original dictionary with the new prefix or suffix.

Questions:
Title: Do C++ compilers remove const variables used only to make code clearer.
Body: <p>Say you have a function</p>
<pre><code>void func(int a, int b, int c, int d);
</code></pre>
<p>Now, when you call this function, because it has a lot of parameters, instead calling it like this:</p>
<pre><code>func(1, 2, 3, 4);
</code></pre>
<p>you might call it like this:</p>
<pre><code>const int a = 1;
const int b = 2;
const int c = 3;
const int d = 4;

func(a, b, c, d);
</code></pre>
<p>to better understand what each value is.</p>
<p>So the question is: do C++ compilers just replace the second way with the first or does it actually allocate space for those filler variables in the stack, wasting both memory and time?</p>

Answers:
```cpp
void func(int a, int b, int c, int d) {
    // If no values are assigned to the variables, the compiler will not allocate space for them
    // This is because the compiler can deduce the values from the function parameters
    // So, the second way of calling the function is equivalent to the first way
}

const int a = 1;
const int b = 2;
const int c = 3;
const int d = 4;

func(a, b, c, d);
```

In this code, the function `func` takes four integer parameters and uses them to perform some operations. The second way of calling the function is equivalent to the first way, which assigns values to the variables `a`, `b`, `c`, and `d`. The compiler deduces the values from the function parameters, so there is no need to allocate space for the variables in the stack.

def anonymous_function(x):
    return x ** 2

def function_handle(x):
    return x ** 2

print(anonymous_function(5))  # Output: 25
print(function_handle(5))   # Output: 25

Questions:
Title: Is there a way to create qualifier-preserving functions in C?.
Body: <p>I have a function that accepts a pointer but does not modify the pointed data, but returns the same pointer or a derivative thereof. I could make the pointer <code>const</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const void *some_read_only_operation(const void *ptr) {
    // Do work with ptr
    return ptr;
}
</code></pre>
<p>However, this means if I call the function, I will not be able to manipulate the result without a cast, even if the pointer I passed was not <code>const</code>. On the contrary, if I make the pointers and return type in the function non <code>const</code> then I will need a cast to pass any pointer to the function that is <code>const</code>. And I will get a non <code>const</code> pointer back so I have broken <code>const</code> safety.</p>
<p>Is there any way to create a function in C that repects <code>const</code> correctness (<code>const</code> in means <code>const</code> out and non <code>const</code> in means non <code>const</code> out) without having to write 2 separate functions? If not, how close can I get?</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> This seems to be the type of question that is likely to have been asked before. I tried to search for duplicates using keywords like <em>const function return</em> or <em>qualifier preserving function</em> but I could not find any.</p>

Answers:
None
```c
// Define a function that returns a pointer to a const-qualified result
void* const_qualifier_preserving_function(void* ptr) {
    // Do work with ptr
    return ptr;
}

// Example usage:
void* const_qualifier_preserving_result = const_qualifier_preserving_function(&some_const_ptr);
```

Questions:
Title: Argument mismatch when there doesn&#39;t seem to be any.
Body: <p>I'm trying to write a program to find the determinant of an NxN matrix, and in doing so, learning about <code>std::array</code> and <code>templates</code> for the first time.</p>
<p>I came up with this implementation, based on the method of cofactors and minor.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

template &lt;int N&gt;
using Matrix = std::array&lt;std::array&lt;double, N&gt;, N&gt;;

template &lt;int N&gt;
Matrix&lt;N - 1&gt; subMatrix(Matrix&lt;N&gt; matrix, size_t focusRow, size_t focusColumn) {
    Matrix&lt;N - 1&gt; returnMatrix;

    int subMatrixRow = 0, subMatrixColumn = 0;
    static const int matrixSize = matrix.size();

    for (size_t matrixRow = 0; matrixRow &lt; matrixSize; matrixRow++) {
        for (size_t matrixColumn = 0; matrixColumn &lt; matrixSize; matrixColumn++) {
            if (matrixRow != focusRow &amp;&amp; matrixColumn != focusColumn) {
                returnMatrix[subMatrixRow][subMatrixColumn++] = matrix[matrixRow][matrixColumn];

                if (subMatrixColumn == matrixSize - 1) {
                    subMatrixColumn = 0;
                    subMatrixRow++;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return returnMatrix;
}

template &lt;int N&gt;
double getDeterminant(Matrix&lt;N&gt; matrix) {
    static const int matrixSize = matrix.size();
    double determinant = 0;

    if (matrixSize == 1) {
        determinant = matrix[0][0];
    }

    else if (matrixSize == 2) {
        determinant = (matrix[0][0] * matrix[1][1]) - (matrix[0][1] * matrix[1][0]);
    }

    else if (matrixSize &gt; 0) {
        int sign = 1;

        for (size_t dimension = 0; dimension &lt; N; dimension++) {
            determinant += sign * matrix[0][dimension] * getDeterminant(subMatrix(matrix, 0, dimension));
            sign = -sign;
        }
    }

    else {
        throw std::invalid_argument(&quot;expected square matrix&quot;);
    }

    return determinant;
}

int main(int argc, char const* argv[]) {
    static const int length = 3;

    Matrix&lt;length&gt; matrix = {{{1, 2, 3},
                              {4, 5, 6},
                              {7, 8, 9}}};

    printf(&quot;determinant = %.2f\n&quot;, getDeterminant(matrix));
}

</code></pre>
<p>However, when I run <code>make</code>, I get an error I'm not quite able to resolve. How to proceed?</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>vscode ➜ /workspaces/c-cpp-mirror/11032023-arrays $ make determinant
g++     determinant.cpp   -o determinant
determinant.cpp: In function ‘int main(int, const char**)’:
determinant.cpp:68:57: error: no matching function for call to ‘getDeterminant(Matrix&lt;3&gt;&amp;)’
   68 |     printf(&quot;determinant = %.2f\n&quot;, getDeterminant(matrix));
      |                                                         ^
determinant.cpp:31:8: note: candidate: ‘template&lt;int N&gt; double getDeterminant(Matrix&lt;N&gt;)’
   31 | double getDeterminant(Matrix&lt;N&gt; matrix) {
      |        ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~
determinant.cpp:31:8: note:   template argument deduction/substitution failed:
determinant.cpp:68:57: note:   mismatched types ‘int’ and ‘long unsigned int’
   68 |     printf(&quot;determinant = %.2f\n&quot;, getDeterminant(matrix));
      |                                                         ^
make: *** [&lt;builtin&gt;: determinant] Error 1
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Here's a smaller program that still exhibits the same behaviour:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

template &lt;int N&gt;
using Matrix = std::array&lt;std::array&lt;double, N&gt;, N&gt;;

template &lt;int N&gt;
void printMatrix(Matrix&lt;N&gt; matrix) {}

int main(int argc, char const* argv[]) {
    static const int length = 4;

    Matrix&lt;length&gt; matrix = {{{7, -2, 2, 1},
                              {3, 1, -5, 2},
                              {2, 2, -5, 3},
                              {3, 2, 5, 1}}};

    printMatrix(matrix);
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
None

The code provided is a Python implementation of the determinant calculation using cofactors and minors. The `getDeterminant` function calculates the determinant of an NxN matrix by recursively breaking down the matrix into smaller sub-matrices, which are then multiplied by their cofactors.

However, the issue lies in the `getDeterminant` function, where the variable `matrix` is declared as `Matrix&lt;N&gt;`, but it is being used as a 2D array (a matrix of arrays). This is because the `Matrix` template is designed to work with 2D arrays, not 1D arrays.

To fix this, the `Matrix` template should be modified to work with 1D arrays by changing the type of the inner arrays to `double`. Here's the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

template <int N>
using Matrix = std::array<double, N>;

template <int N>
double getDeterminant(Matrix<N> matrix) {
    static const int matrixSize = matrix.size();
    double determinant = 0;

    if (matrixSize == 1) {
        determinant = matrix[0][0];
    }

    else if (matrixSize == 2) {
        determinant = (matrix[0][0] * matrix[1][1]) - (matrix[0][1] * matrix[1][0]);
    }

    else if (matrixSize > 0) {
        int sign = 1;

        for (size_t dimension = 0; dimension < N; dimension++) {
            determinant += sign * matrix[0][dimension] * getDeterminant(std::array<double, N - 1>(std::array<double, N >{matrix[0][dimension], matrix[1][dimension]})));
            sign = -sign;
        }
    }

    else {
        throw std::invalid_argument("expected square matrix");
    }

    return determinant;
}

int main(int argc, char const* argv[]) {
    static const int length = 4;

    Matrix<4> matrix = {{7, -2, 2, 1},
                         {3, 1, -5, 2},
                         {2, 2, -5, 3},
                         {3, 2, 5, 1}};

    std::cout << "Determinant: " << getDeterminant(matrix) << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

None
In C++, loops as in the example below can be endless-loops without side effects, which are undefined behavior, whereas they are not in C:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    // do nothing
}
```
This loop will continue to execute indefinitely, consuming system resources without any apparent purpose.

Questions:
Title: Generally multithreading a for loop in Java.
Body: <p>Suppose I am given integers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> such that a range of interest is formed, of integers in <code>[a,b]</code>. The range can span well over <code>10^9</code> integers. I want to sum the values of a given function <code>f : N -&gt; N</code> over all integers <code>a &lt;= n &lt;= b</code>. The range is very large so I want to do this using multithreading.</p>
<p>Less formaly, I want to parallelize the following code:</p>
<pre><code>long sum = 0;

for (long n = a ; n &lt;= b ; n++)
    sum += f(n);

System.out.println(sum);
</code></pre>
<p>Ideally (at least in my mind), the range will divided equally across the available number of threads available by the processor (suppose <code>f(n)</code> has near-identical complexity and running time for each <code>n</code> in range). The values are completely independent, and <code>f</code> could really be any function. For example, it could output the sum of digits of the number, but it really could be anything, it's just an example.</p>
<p>Is there a general way to do exactly that in Java using multithreading?</p>

Answers:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class MultithreadedSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 1000000; // range of interest
        int b = 2000000; // end of range
        int numThreads = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(); // number of available threads
        int[] f = new int[a + 1]; // function to sum

        // initialize the function values
        for (int i = 0; i <= a; i++) {
            f[i] = i;
        }

        // divide the range into chunks using multithreading
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(numThreads);
        int chunkSize = Math.min(a, b) / numThreads;
        int chunkStart = a - chunkSize;
        int chunkEnd = Math.min(chunkStart + chunkSize, b);

        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
            int start = i * chunkSize + chunkStart;
            int end = Math.min((i + 1) * chunkSize, chunkEnd);
            executor.submit(() -> {
                for (int n = start; n < end; n++) {
                    f[n] = f[n] + f[n - chunkSize];
                }
            });
        }

        // sum the values in parallel
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads; i++) {
            sum += f[i];
        }

        System.out.println(sum);
    }
}
```

