Title: How to iterate over solid-js children with typescript annotations.
Body: <p>Say I have the following component, how would I iterate over and render each child to do stuff like wrapping each child with other components?</p>
<pre><code>interface StuffProps {
  children: `?`
}

function Stuff({ children }: StuffProps) {
  // ?
}
</code></pre>
<p>I've tried setting <code>children: JSX.Element</code> and then doing <code>&lt;For each={children}&gt; ... &lt;/For&gt;</code> but it gives me a typescript error.</p>

Answers:
None

To iterate over the children of a SolidJS component, you can use the `React.Children` utility from the React library. Here's an example of how you can do it:

```typescript
import React from 'react';

interface StuffProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

function Stuff({ children }: StuffProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      {React.Children.map(children, (child) => (
        <div key={child}>{child}</div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this code, `React.Children.map` takes an array of children and returns a new array with the children rendered as JSX elements. The `key` prop is set to the child's type to ensure that the array is rendered in the correct order.

