Questions:
Title: Does C# have a short-hand syntax for creating key/value pairs?.
Body: <p>I have a C# method declared like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public void Process&lt;K, V&gt;(params KeyValuePair&lt;K, V&gt;[] items)
{
    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage of this method looks kind of ugly; for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Process(
    new KeyValuePair(&quot;key&quot;, &quot;value&quot;),
    new KeyValuePair(123, Guid.NewGuid())
);
</code></pre>
<p>In Kotlin, you can create pairs using the <code>to</code> infix function; for example:</p>
<pre><code>val pair1 = &quot;key&quot; to &quot;value&quot;
val pair2 = 123 to UUID.randomUUID()
</code></pre>
<p>So the equivalent method usage looks a little tidier; for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>process(&quot;key&quot; to &quot;value&quot;, 123 to UUID.randomUUID())
</code></pre>
<p>C# doesn't have infix functions, but something nearly equivalent could be achieved with extension methods; for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static KeyValuePair&lt;K, V&gt; To&lt;K, V&gt;(this K key, V value) where K : notnull
{
    return new KeyValuePair(key, value);
}

var pair1 = &quot;key&quot;.To(&quot;value&quot;);
var pair2 = 123.To(Guid.NewGuid());

Process(&quot;key&quot;.To(&quot;value&quot;), 123.To(Guid.NewGuid()));
</code></pre>
<p>That doesn't seem like the most elegant solution, so the other thing I was considering was that C# has dictionary initializer syntax; for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>new Dictionary&lt;object, object&gt;()
{
    [&quot;key&quot;] = &quot;value&quot;,
    [123] = Guid.NewGuid()
}
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>new Dictionary&lt;object, object&gt;()
{
    { &quot;key&quot;, &quot;value&quot; },
    { 123, Guid.NewGuid() }
}
</code></pre>
<p>So I was wondering whether dictionary initializer syntax could be applied to a method parameter; for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Process({ [&quot;key&quot;] = &quot;value&quot;, [123] = Guid.NewGuid() });
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Process({{ &quot;key&quot;, &quot;value&quot; }, { 123, Guid.NewGuid() }});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Questions</strong></p>
<p>Is dictionary initializer syntax as a method parameter possible, or is it syntactic sugar provided by the compiler when using a dictionary?</p>
<p>Are there any other elegant ways to create <code>params</code> of <code>KeyValuePair&lt;K, V&gt;</code>?</p>

Answers:
```python
def key_collection_asIEnumerable():
    # Create a list of strings
    x = ["hello", "world"]

    # Try to use Contains method on a string
    try:
        print("Contains method works", x[0].Contains("w"))
    except TypeError:
        print("Contains method throws TypeError")

    # Try to use ContainsKey method on a string
    try:
        print("ContainsKey method works", x[0].Contains_key("w"))
    except TypeError:
        print("ContainsKey method throws TypeError")

    # Try to use Contains method on a dictionary
    try:
        print("Contains method works", "hello".get("w", None).Contains("w"))
    except KeyError:
        print("Contains method throws KeyError")

    # Try to use ContainsKey method on a dictionary
    try:
        print("ContainsKey method works", "hello".get("w", None).get_key("w", None).Contains_key("w"))
    except KeyError:
        print("ContainsKey method throws KeyError")

    return None

key_collection_asIEnumerable()
```

