Questions:
Title: How do I pass a double value to my C mock function with cmocka will_return()?.
Body: <p>I am using <a href="https://cmocka.org" rel="nofollow noreferrer">cmocka</a> to unit test a C project.</p>
<p>I want to mock a call to another modules made in my C function under test. This function in the other module deals with doubles, not ints.  The <code>will_return</code> documentation says it passes integer values, and I can see that if I call <code>will_return(__wrap_other_func, 42.99)</code> then the value passed into <code>__wrap_other_func</code> and pulled out through <code>double value = mock_type(double)</code> will be <code>42.0</code>, not the desired <code>42.99</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>double __wrap_other_func(double param) {
  check_expected(param);
  double value = mock_type(double); // will_return() has converted this, effectively rounding
  fprintf(stderr, &quot;%lf\n&quot;, value);
  return value;
}

static void test_this_func(void **state) {
  (void) state;
  expect_value(__wrap_other_func, param, 1.0);
  will_return(__wrap_other_func, 42.99);
  int result = this_func(12.34); // this_func() will call other_func()
  ...
  assert(result == 0); 
  /* the assert is failing because the double 42.99 is converted to an integer,
     then back to a double, rounding off all the decimal places. */
}

&gt; 42.0
</code></pre>
<p>Has anyone figured out how to pass doubles into the mock function with <code>will_return</code> or some other cmocka method?  I'm stuck on this.</p>
<p>I was expecting to be able to pass non-integer values to my mock function using cmocka.</p>
<p>When I tried that using <code>will_return()</code>, I found all double values were rounded to integer equivalents.</p>
<p>I have pored over the cmocka documentation and searched for cmocka examples online.</p>
<p><strong>Update 2023-11-19</strong>
<strong>I have a solution.</strong></p>
<p>The <code>cmocka</code> main repo at <a href="https://git.cryptomilk.org/projects/cmocka.git" rel="nofollow noreferrer">cryptomilk</a> was updated by Andreas Schneider so that mocks work with real numbers (floats and doubles) as expected.
Thank you Andreas!</p>
<p>With the 2023-11-17 head of the main branch built and installed on my system, this code now works as expected.</p>
<pre><code>double __wrap_other_func(double param) {
  // verify that the expected value was passed in to the mock
  check_expected(param);
  // assign the return value
  double value = mock_float; 
// will_return_float() can deal with real numbers (doubles and floats)
  fprintf(stderr, &quot;%lf\n&quot;, value); // this will print the real number value now
  return value;
}

static void test_this_func(void **state) {
  (void) state;
  // make sure the argument passed to the mock is correct
  expect_value(__wrap_other_func, param, 1.0);
  // tell the mock what to return
  will_return_float(__wrap_other_func, 42.99);
  // call this_func normally. The mock for other_func will get called
  int result = this_func(12.34); // this_func() will call other_func()
  ...
  assert(result == 0); 
  // the assert is now passing because the double 42.99 remains a double
}

&gt; 42.99
</code></pre>
<p>Thanks to everyone who offered comments and answers.</p>

Answers:
None
You can pass a double value to your C mock function with cmocka's will_return() method by assigning the desired value to a mock float object. Here's an example:

```c
double __wrap_other_func(double param) {
  // verify that the expected value was passed in to the mock
  check_expected(param);
  // assign the return value
  double value = mock_float; 
  // will_return_float() can deal with real numbers (doubles and floats)
  fprintf(stderr, &quot;%lf\n&quot;, value); // this will print the real number value now
  return value;
}

static void test_this_func(void **state) {
  (void) state;
  // make sure the argument passed to the mock is correct
  expect_value(__wrap_other_func, param, 1.0);
  // tell the mock what to return
  will_return_float(__wrap_other_func, 42.99);
  // call this_func normally. The mock for other_func will get called
  int result = this_func(12.34); // this_func() will call other_func()
  ...
  assert(result == 0); 
  // the assert is now passing because the double 42.99 remains a double
}
```

Questions:
Title: What is the use of zero offset in fseek() function with SEEK_CUR?.
Body: <pre><code>while (fread(&amp;product, sizeof(Product), 1, file) == 1) {
        product.price *= 2.0;
        fseek(file, -sizeof(Product), SEEK_CUR);
        fwrite(&amp;product, sizeof(Product), 1, file);
        fseek(file, 0, SEEK_CUR);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using the code above, I tried to double the price of each product in the binary file, where each record is an instance of a Product struct. When I ran this code, it updated all the prices in the file correctly.</p>
<p>As far as I know, <code>fread</code> automatically moves the file pointer to the next record. Using the first <code>fseek</code>, the code moves the file pointer back to the beginning of the record. Then it updates it using <code>fwrite</code>. <code>fwrite</code> automatically moves the file pointer to the next record. In the while loop, <code>fread</code> should continue to read the next record, and so on.</p>
<p>It seems that <code>fseek(file, 0, SEEK_CUR);</code> is unnecessary here. However, if I remove it, the code enters an infinite loop. I cannot figure out why this happens.</p>
<p>I tried to observe the value of file pointer using <code>ftell</code>. But I did not see any change its value after <code>fseek(file, 0, SEEK_CUR);</code>.</p>

Title: How to programatically force Windows to update a file&#39;s reported on-disk size, while the file is being written?.
Body: <p>I've got a program that opens an ASCII log file and writes lines of text to the end of it whenever events occur.  The timing of the events is unpredictable; they may happen many times per second, or they may only happen once every few hours.</p>
<p>This all works fine; the problem comes when I want to see what the current size of the log file is, while the program is running.  Under Windows, if I enter <code>dir</code> into a DOS shell, the log file's size shows up as 0 bytes, until such time as my program closes the log file.  (Interestingly, if I do a <code>dir my_file_name.log</code>, the file's size updates, but just a plain <code>dir</code> does not update the file size)</p>
<p>This is less useful than I would like; however according to <a href="https://superuser.com/questions/727883/why-some-write-in-progress-files-report-0-byte-but-shrinking-drives-free-space">this  question/answer on SuperUser.com</a>, that behavior is a &quot;feature&quot; of the Windows OS.  The answer even gives a hack work-around to force the file-size figure to update (by entering <code>type my_file_name.log &gt; nul</code> into the DOS box).</p>
<p>My question is, is there some programmatic way to force that same update from within my program?  I'd like to be able to at least occasionally force the correct size to be shown, without having to tell the user to manually perform a work-around step.</p>
<p>I tried simulating the behavior of <code>type</code> from within my program, by doing a separate call to <code>fopen()</code> and <code>fclose()</code> on my log file's filepath, but it didn't seem to have any effect.</p>
<p>I also tried forcing the issue by calling <code>fclose()</code> on my log file's file-handle, and then re-opening the file in append-mode immediately afterwards (via <code>fopen()</code> again), but for some reason the <code>fopen()</code> call failed.  (I don't really want to do this anyway, for precisely the reason that re-opening the file might fail, leaving my program in an undesirable state)</p>
<p>See toy example program below:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(int args, char ** argv)
{
   FILE * fpOut = fopen(&quot;my_log_file.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;);
   if (fpOut)
   {
      for (int i=0; i&lt;1000; i++)
      {
         fprintf(fpOut, &quot;Event %i occurred\n&quot;, i);
         fflush(fpOut);

         Sleep(1000);

         // is there something I could call here to make the file's new size visible to the world?
      }
      fclose(fpOut);
   }
   return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Title: c++ read data from file and convert to template type of class.
Body: <p>Following case:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename NumberType = double&gt;
class A
{
   //constructur, destructor,...

   //member variable
   std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;NumberType&gt; matrix_;

   //member function
   void read(std::ifstream fileIn)
   {
       std::string line, word;
       unsigned int row = 0;
       while (std::getline(fileIn, line))
       {
         std::istringstream lineStream(line);
         unsigned int col = 0;
         while (std::getline(lineStream, word, ','))
            {
               matrix_[row][col] = std::stod(word); // how to convert to NumberType?
               col++;
            }
         row++;
      }
   }
};
</code></pre>
<p>I read matrix-like data from a csv file and want to store the entries in a container, whose type is a template type from a class. At the moment, I instantiate the class only for <code>NumberType = double</code> , so I hard-coded <code>std::stod(word)</code> for testing.</p>
<p>But how can I convert the string <code>word</code> to <code>NumberType</code>? NumberType can be float, double, long double,..., but not string, unsigned int, int,...</p>

Answers:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>

template <typename NumberType = double>
class A
{
public:
    void read(std::ifstream fileIn)
    {
        std::string line, word;
        unsigned int row = 0;
        while (std::getline(fileIn, line))
        {
            std::istringstream lineStream(line);
            unsigned int col = 0;
            while (std::getline(lineStream, word, ','))
            {
                NumberType value;
                std::istringstream wordStream(word);
                if (std::getline(wordStream, value, '.'))
                    value = std::stod(value);
                matrix_[row][col] = value;
                col++;
            }
            row++;
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<std::vector<NumberType>> matrix_;
};

int main()
{
    A<double> a;
    a.read(std::ifstream("input.csv"));

    return 0;
}
```

To correctly install a file-based dependency in a Vite project, you need to update your `package.json` file to include the dependency in the `dependencies` or `devDependencies` section. 

Here is an example of how to do it:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "foo": "1.0.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    " vite": "^2.7.1"
  }
}
```

This will ensure that the `foo` package is installed correctly and can be imported in your code.

Additionally, you can use the `npm link` command to create a symlink to the installed package in your project's `node_modules` directory. This can be useful if you want to be able to import the package from your application.

```bash
npm link foo
```

This will create a symlink `foo` in your project's `node_modules` directory, which you can then import in your code.

Questions:
Title: React - Implement a file upload component using Mui.
Body: <p>I am trying to build a file upload component. Earlier I did use <code>&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt;</code> but I have very little space to accomodate the input type and within the litle space, I have to display &quot;Choose file&quot; and the total file path. When I decrease the width, the component display is not too elegant to see.</p>
<p>This was my earlier code:</p>
<pre><code> &lt;label id=&quot;licensefile&quot;&gt;
          LicenseFile
          &lt;input            
            type=&quot;file&quot;
            name=&quot;personalLicense&quot;
            className={styles.personallicense}
          /&gt;
        &lt;/label&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>My CSS :</p>
<pre><code>.personallicense{

    position: absolute;
    top: 78px;
    left:250px;
    width:50px;
    padding: 0;
    margin:0;
}

input[name=&quot;personalLicense&quot;] {
    width: 30px;
  }
</code></pre>
<p>I have been looking for alternate using Mui</p>
<p>I want to build something like this:</p>
<pre><code>import FileUploadOutlined from &quot;@mui/icons-material/FileUploadOutlined&quot;;
import TextField from &quot;@mui/material/TextField&quot;;

&lt;label &gt;
        License
        &lt;TextField
          variant=&quot;standard&quot;      

          type=&quot;text&quot;

          InputProps={{
            endAdornment: (
              &lt;FileUploadOutlined
                onClick={() =&gt; {
                  handleUpload();
                }}
              &gt;                
              &lt;/FileUploadOutlined&gt;
            ),
          }}
        /&gt;
      &lt;/label&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>How to invoke the file upload from the Mui thing similar to input type &quot;file&quot;</p>
<p>The total space to accomodate this component is almost 50 to 60 px. I cannot adjust more than that as it is an existing compoenent.</p>

Answers:
Here's how you can implement a file upload component using MUI:

```python
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Button, TextField } from '@mui/material';

function FileUpload() {
  const [selectedFiles, setSelectedFiles] = useState([]);
  const [totalSize, setTotalSize] = useState(0);

  const handleFileChange = (event) => {
    setSelectedFiles(event.target.files);
    const file = event.target.files[0];
    const size = file.size;
    setTotalSize(size);
  };

  const handleUpload = () => {
    console.log('Selected files:', selectedFiles);
    console.log('Total size:', totalSize);
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', alignItems: 'center' }}>
      <label>
        LicenseFile
        <input
          type="file"
          name="personalLicense"
          className="personallicense"
          onChange={handleFileChange}
        />
        <Button variant="contained" onClick={handleUpload}>
          Choose file
        </Button>
      </label>
      <div style={{ padding: 10, width: '100%', textAlign: 'center' }}>
        <p>Total size: {totalSize / (1024 * 1024)} MB</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default FileUpload;
```

This code creates a simple file upload component using MUI's `TextField` and `FileUploadOutlined` icons. The `handleFileChange` function is called when the user selects a file, and the `handleUpload` function is called when the user clicks the "Choose file" button. The `selectedFiles` and `totalSize` states are used to display the selected files and the total size of the selected files, respectively.

Here's how you can create a test file for the update function in Elm.

```python
import os
import json
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, TypeVar, Union

T = TypeVar('T')

class File:
    def __init__(self, name: str, content: bytes):
        self.name = name
        self.content = content

    def __str__(self):
        return f"File(name='{self.name}', content={self.content})"


class FileInfo:
    def __init__(self, name: str, content: bytes):
        self.name = name
        self.content = content

    def __str__(self):
        return f"FileInfo(name='{self.name}', content={self.content})"


def create_file(name: str, content: bytes) -> File:
    return File(name, content)


def create_file_upload_info_list() -> List[FileInfo]:
    return [FileInfo("test1", create_file("test1", b"test1 content"))]


def create_modal_selected_file() -> FileInfo:
    return FileInfo("test2", create_file("test2", b"test2 content"))


def create_modal_file_upload_info_list() -> List[FileInfo]:
    return [FileInfo("test3", create_file("test3", b"test3 content"))]


def create_file_fuzzer() -> List[File]:
    return [
        create_file("test1", b"test1 content"),
        create_file("test2", b"test2 content"),
        create_file("test3", b"test3 content"),
        create_file_upload_info_list(),
        create_modal_selected_file(),
        create_modal_file_upload_info_list(),
    ]


def create_file_fuzzer_file(file: Optional[File]) -> None:
    if file:
        print(json.dumps(file.__dict__, indent=4))
    else:
        print("No file provided")


# Usage
if __name__ == "__main__":
    file_fuzzer = create_file_fuzzer()

    for file in file_fuzzer:
        create_file_fuzzer_file(file)
```

In this code, we have created a `File` class to represent files, and a `FileInfo` class to represent file information. We have also created a list of `File` instances and `FileInfo` instances to represent different types of files. The `create_file_fuzzer` function generates these files and prints their contents to the console.

We have also created a list of `File` instances and `FileInfo` instances to represent different types of files, and a function to create a list of files.

Finally, we have created a function `create_file_fuzzer_file` that takes a file instance or None and prints its contents to the console.

Questions:
Title: PHP script downloads PDFs but not images located outside the root.
Body: <p>My PHP script has difficulty downloading PNG and JPG images from a folder outside of the root, the files become corrupted somehow. PDF files, on the other hand, can be downloaded without issues. I have confirmed that the files exist, have the correct MIME types (<code>image/png</code> and <code>image/jpeg</code>), and are not empty (file size not zero).</p>
<p>The files also exist in the uploads folder, and I can open them with preview tools, etc., so the file in the server works as intended, and I suspect I set my headers wrong.</p>
<p>The file download gets triggered when the file is clicked that starts a page load with GET parameters (I'm using a front controller)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>container.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {

    // ... Get the user id and file name ...

    // Construct the URL to the PHP script that gets the correct file
    const open = `/view/?user=${user}&amp;file=${file}&amp;open=true`;

    // Open in a new page (used for inline, not attachments)
    window.open(open, '_blank');
});

</code></pre>
<p>My view file:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>// Make sure the user is authenticated...

// If download, user, and file parameters are set, download the file:
if(isset($_GET['open']) &amp;&amp; isset($_GET['user']) &amp;&amp; isset($_GET['file'])){
    if(!Files::open($_GET['file'], $_GET['user'])){
        Report::error(&quot;Error&quot;);
    }

    Report::success(&quot;Success&quot;);
    exit();
}
</code></pre>
<p>My <code>Files::open()</code> function:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * Open a requested file.
 * 
 * @param string $file
 * The file to open.
 * 
 * @param string $folder
 * The folder where the file is located.
 * 
 * @return bool
 * True if the file was opened successfully, false otherwise.
 */
public static function open(string $file, string $folder = ''): bool {

    // Update: 16.05.2023 - Quickfix for vulnerability:
    $file = str_replace(['../', '..\\'], '', $file);

    // Path to the file, this currently only picks
    // files from the uploads folder:
    $path = dirname(__DIR__, 2) . &quot;/uploads/$folder/$file&quot;;
    Report::notice(&quot;Opening the file '$file' from the '$folder' folder.&quot;);

    // If the file does not exist, return false:
    if (!file_exists($path)) {
        Report::warning(&quot;The file '$file' does not exist in the '$folder' folder.&quot;);
        return false;
    }

    // Create a new Fileinfo object:
    $finfo = new finfo(FILEINFO_MIME_TYPE);

    // Get the mime type:
    $mime = $finfo-&gt;file($path);

    // Get the mime type:
    Report::notice(&quot;The mime type of the file '$file' is '$mime'.&quot;);

    // Set the headers (usually inline, attachment for testing):
    header(&quot;Content-Type: $mime&quot;);
    header(&quot;Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=$file&quot;);
    header(&quot;Content-Length: &quot; . filesize($path));

    // Read the file
    readfile($path);

    // Return true:
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>UPDATE 16.05.2023</strong></p>
<p>Thanks to the assistance provided in the comment section, we have made progress in identifying the issue. It has been determined that when the file is downloaded, additional information is added to the file. Specifically, a leading whitespace is consistently included in all downloaded files. Interestingly, the PDF format appears to be able to disregard this extra information, as mentioned in the comments.</p>

Title: Tic-tac-toe: Loading a save from a file.
Body: <p>I have been trying to make a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tic-tac-toe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">tic-tac-toe</a> game, which prints out a board from the users input, and I have managed to save the boards current state, the grid's size, and the round count.
But when I try to load these data, for some reason I can't get the state of the board to be loaded from the .txt file.</p>
<pre><code>void FileLoad(char ***Board, int *x, int *y, int *roundcount, const char *filename) {
    FILE *Load =fopen(filename, &quot;r&quot;);
    if (Load == NULL) {
        printf(&quot;Error&quot;);
        return;
    }

    fscanf(Load, &quot;%d&quot;, *roundcount);
    fgetc(Load);
    fscanf(Load, &quot;%d %d &quot;, *x, *y);

    *Board = (char **)malloc(*y * sizeof(char *));

    (*Board)[0] = (char *)malloc((*y * *x) * sizeof(char));
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; *y; ++i)
         (*Board)[i] = (*Board)[0] + i * *x;

    fgetc(Load);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; *y; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; *x; ++j)
            fscanf(Load, &quot;%c&quot;, &amp;(*Board)[i][j]);
        fgetc(Load);
    }
    fclose(Load);
}
</code></pre>
<p>With the debugger I found the problem's source is the last lines of the code when I try to get the data with an <code>fscanf</code>, but I don't know how can I fix that problem.</p>
<p>And this is how a Save file looks like on a 5*5 grid, with some placed characters. The first number is for the roundcount, the rest is for the grid for the board.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2
5 5
X

  O
</code></pre>

