Questions:
Title: Why does a C style &#39;class&#39; result in extra instructions?.
Body: <p>Comparing C style struct with 'member' functions to a C++ class in an attempt to model C++ overheads, I suspected that the following implementation would be roughly equivlent by containing the same number of instructions. I see that the C implementation results in extra instuctions when calling the 'member' functions.</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    uint32_t a;
    rectangle_t r;

    /* struct */
    r.set(2,3, &amp;r);
    /* asm 
       ldr     r3, [r7, #12]
       adds    r2, r7, #4
       movs    r1, #3
       movs    r0, #2
       blx     r3
    */

    a = r.getArea(&amp;r);
    /* asm 
       ldr     r3, [r7, #16]
       adds    r2, r7, #4
       mov     r0, r2
       blx     r3
       str     r0, [r7, #20]
       movs    r3, #0
    */

    /* Class */
    r.set(2, 3);
    /* asm 
       adds    r3, r7, #4
       movs    r2, #3
       movs    r1, #2
       mov     r0, r3
       bl      rectangle_t::set(unsigned int, unsigned int)
    */
    a = r.getArea();
    /* asm 
       adds    r3, r7, #4
       mov     r0, r3
       bl      rectangle_t::getArea()
       str     r0, [r7, #12]
       movs    r3, #0
    */
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why is there an extra ldr instruction when calling a stuct 'member' vs a class member?</p>
<p>Compiler: ARM GCC 12.2.0 (linux)</p>
<p>Declarations:</p>
<pre><code>typedef struct rectangle rectangle_t;
struct rectangle
{
    uint32_t w;
    uint32_t l;
    void (*set)(uint32_t L, uint32_t W, rectangle_t *self);
    uint32_t (*getArea)(rectangle_t *self);    
};
void set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W, rectangle_t *self)
{
    self-&gt;w = W;
    self-&gt;l = L;
}

uint32_t getArea(rectangle_t *self)
{
    return self-&gt;l * self-&gt;w;
}

class rectangle_t
{
public:   

    uint32_t w;
    uint32_t l;  
    void set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W);
    uint32_t getArea(); 
};

void rectangle_t::set(uint32_t L, uint32_t W)
{
    w = W;
    l = L;
}

uint32_t rectangle_t::getArea()
{
    return l * w;
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
None
Here is the equivalent code for the provided C++ class:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>

class Rectangle {
public:
    uint32_t width;
    uint32_t length;
    void (*set)(uint32_t length, uint32_t width);
    uint32_t getArea() {
        return length * width;
    }
};

void set(uint32_t length, uint32_t width) {
    width = length;
    length = 0;
}

uint32_t getArea() {
    return 0;
}
```

Questions:
Title: Why is a private member visible when initializing a static variable?.
Body: <p>This compiles:</p>
<pre><code>class Node{
    int m = 0;

    static unsigned f(){
        return 1;
    }

public:
    static unsigned a;
    static unsigned b;
};

unsigned Node::a = sizeof(m); // &lt;= !!!
unsigned Node::b = f();       // &lt;= !!!

int main(){
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why? I know <code>sizeof</code> is not function but still, aren't <code>m</code> and <code>f()</code> private?</p>
<p>It is definitely not a bug, because the code compiles on GCC, MSVC, Clang and Intel C++.</p>
<p>Update: Also, note that it is just <code>sizeof(m)</code> and not <code>sizeof(Node::m)</code>; same with <code>f()</code> instead of <code>Node::f()</code>. (Koenig's lookup?)</p>

Answers:
None
class Node {
    int m = 0;

    static unsigned f(){
        return 1;
    }

public:
    static unsigned a;
    static unsigned b;
};

unsigned Node::a = sizeof(m); // &lt;= !!!
unsigned Node::b = f();       // &lt;= !!!

int main(){
}

Questions:
Title: same method or variable name in a javascript class behaves differently.
Body: <pre><code>class Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method);
</code></pre>
<p>console return method in child class which is a expected behaviour.</p>
<pre><code>class Parent{
    method = &quot;sss&quot;
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Child&quot;)
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method)
</code></pre>
<p>why does the console return method variable - &quot;sss&quot; in Parent class ?</p>

Answers:
```python
class Parent:
    def method(self):
        print("Parent")

class Child(Parent):
    def method(self):
        print("Child")

child = Child()
print(child.method())  # Outputs: Child
```

When the code is run, it creates an instance of the `Child` class and calls the `method` method on it. The `method` method in the `Parent` class has a method parameter `self`, which is a reference to the instance of the class. This is the same as in JavaScript, where a function can take an object as an argument.

In the `Child` class, the `method` method has the same name as a method in the `Parent` class, which means Python will look for a method with that name in the `Child` class's namespace. Since the `method` method in the `Parent` class has a different name (`self.method`), Python will look for a method with that name in the `Child` class's namespace, which is why it returns the `method` method from the `Parent` class.

This is an example of a technique called "method overloading" or "method overriding", where a class can provide multiple methods with the same name, but different parameters or behaviors.

Questions:
Title: Why does the `instanceof` operator return false on instance passed to library? (No inheritance involved).
Body: <p>I am building a library with TS. This library uses the <code>ssh2</code> library as a dependency.</p>
<p>I'm trying to create a function that can either accept an <code>ssh2</code> configuration object or an already existing <code>Client</code> instance to execute a command (this is a simplified case):</p>
<pre><code>import { Client, ConnectConfig } from 'ssh2';

export function runCommand(params: Client | ConnectConfig) {
  if(params instanceof Client) {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>When I build this library and call the function like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { readFileSync } = require(&quot;fs&quot;);
const { Client } = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
const { runCommand } = require(&quot;myLib&quot;);
const conn = new Client();

conn
  .on(&quot;ready&quot;, () =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Client :: ready&quot;);
    runCommand(conn);
  })
  .connect({
    host: &quot;example.com&quot;,
    port: 22,
    username: &quot;me&quot;,
    privateKey: readFileSync(process.env.HOME + &quot;/.ssh/id_ed25519&quot;),
  });
</code></pre>
<p>the <code>instanceof</code> check in my function will return <code>false</code> for some reason. What exactly is happening here? Is it a TS compilation issue or does Node.js see the <code>Client</code> from my lib's dependency and my consumer code's dependency as two different classes?</p>
<p>The compiled code looks something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const ssh2_1 = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
//...
function runCommand(params) {
    if (params instanceof ssh2_1.Client) {
        // do something
    }
    else {
       // create our own client
    // do something
    }
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
You are correct that the `instanceof` operator in JavaScript returns `false` when you pass a value that is not an instance of the class it is checking against. This is because `instanceof` checks if the value is an instance of the class, and if not, it immediately returns `false`.

In your case, the issue is not with TypeScript compilation or Node.js seeing the `Client` from your lib's dependency and your consumer code's dependency as two different classes. The problem lies in the fact that the `Client` type from the `ssh2` library is not a class, but rather an interface or a type definition. It is not a class that can be instantiated.

When you use `instanceof` on a value that is not an instance of the `Client` type, it returns `false` because it is checking if the value is an instance of the `Client` type, but it is not checking if the value is an instance of the class that implements the `Client` interface.

To fix this issue, you can use the `instanceof` operator on the value itself, like this:
```javascript
function runCommand(params) {
  if (params instanceof ssh2.Client) {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
```
Alternatively, you can use the `typeof` operator to check if the value is an instance of the `Client` type, like this:
```javascript
function runCommand(params) {
  if (typeof params === 'function' && params instanceof ssh2.Client) {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
```
This way, you can check if the value is an instance of the `Client` type without having to know the class name.

