Questions:
Title: How to check if date picker is opened or closed in view SwiftUI. without Tap Gesture on Date picker.
Body: <p>When the user hits date picker in swiftUI, it opens up and shows calendar to choose date user may or may not choose date and user hits somewhere else in the view and date picker closes it self. I want to change date picker parent view border when user hits date picker and also when user tap somewhere else screen date picker closes and its parent view border does not changes.I want to handle the date picker parent view border color. when then date picker is closed or dismissed.<a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/vX0Bv.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/vX0Bv.png" alt="this date picker in parent view" /></a> <a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/e3nF1.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/e3nF1.png" alt="changing date picker parent view border color on tap gesture" /></a><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/om89W.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/om89W.png" alt="date picker dismissed/closed but unable to detect when date picker is closed. " /></a></p>
<pre><code>DatePicker(&quot;&quot;, selection: $datePicker, displayedComponents: .date)
                    .labelsHidden()
                    .padding(.trailing, 8)
                    .frame(alignment: .leading)
                    .colorMultiply(AppColors.textNavy100Color)
                    .accentColor(AppColors.textNavy100Color)
                    .onTapGesture {
                        print(&quot;date picker tapped&quot;)
                        if isFieldEditing == false{
                            isFieldEditing = true
                        }else {
                           isFieldEditing = false
                        }
                    }
</code></pre>

Answers:
```python
import SwiftUI

struct DatePickerView: View {
    @State private var isFieldEditing = false
    @State private var datePicker: Date = Date()
    @State private var isDatePickerOpen = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            if isDatePickerOpen {
                Text("Date: \(String(format: "%d-%m-%Y", datePicker))")
                    .padding(.bottom)
            } else {
                Text("Date: \(String(format: "%d-%m-%Y", datePicker))")
                    .padding(.bottom, 16)
            }
            .onTapGesture {
                isDatePickerOpen = true
                isFieldEditing = false
                datePicker = Date()
            }
            .sheet(isPresented: $isDatePickerOpen) {
                DatePicker(selection: $datePicker, displayedComponents: .date)
                    .labelsHidden()
                    .padding(.trailing, 8)
                    .frame(alignment: .leading)
                    .colorMultiply(AppColors.textNavy100Color)
                    .accentColor(AppColors.textNavy100Color)
                    .onTapGesture {
                        isDatePickerOpen = false
                        isFieldEditing = true
                        isDatePickerOpen = true
                        datePicker = Date()
                    }
            }
        }
    }
}

struct DatePickerView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        DatePickerView()
    }
}
```

This code creates a `DatePickerView` that displays a date picker in a SwiftUI view. When the user taps on the date picker, it opens up and displays a calendar. When the user taps somewhere else on the screen, the date picker closes and its parent view's border color changes to `AppColors.textNavy100Color`. The code also includes a `@State` variable to track whether the date picker is open and whether the user is editing the date. The date picker's border color is set when the user taps on it, and it is set to `AppColors.textNavy100Color` when the date picker is closed.

Questions:
Title: How to get users click in calendar view to be stored as dd/mm/yyyy in SQLite.
Body: <p>In my SQLite database I have my dates stored in this format 02/01/2023 but when I try to get the date from the calendarview it gives them to me as 2/1/2023 which causes me to have an error when trying to retrieve the data from the database that matches that date.</p>
<p>How do I format this into a String variable?</p>
<p>My attempt so far:</p>
<pre><code>    public void onSelectedDayChange(@NonNull CalendarView calendarview, int i, int i1,int i2) {
        exercise_name = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        set_weight = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        set_reps = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        set_date= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    //formatting to dd/mm/yyyy
            System.out.println(&quot;both are over 10&quot;);
            date =    i2 + &quot;/&quot; + (i1+1) + &quot;/&quot; + i;

        //month needs plus 1 because it starts at 0
        tviewdate.setText(date);
        storeDataDateinArrays();
</code></pre>

Answers:
To format the date into a string in the format dd/mm/yyyy, you can use the following code:

```java
public void onSelectedDayChange(@NonNull CalendarView calendarview, int i, int i1, int i2) {
    exercise_name = new ArrayList<>();
    set_weight = new ArrayList<>();
    set_reps = new ArrayList<>();
    set_date = new ArrayList<>();

    String date = String.format("%d/%02d/%04d", i2, i1 + 1, i);
    tviewdate.setText(date);

    storeDataDateinArrays();
}
```

In this code, `String.format("%d/%02d/%04d", i2, i1 + 1, i)` formats the date as dd/mm/yyyy. `%d` represents the day of the month, `%02d` pads the day with a leading zero if it's less than 10, and `%04d` pads the day with a leading zero if it's 10 or more.

Questions:
Title: Java DateTimeFormatter Not Working for Pattern &#39;ddMyyyy&#39;.
Body: <p>Consider the following date:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>String date = &quot;2391995&quot;;
String patt = &quot;ddMyyyy&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Using the pattern provided, we can see that <code>date</code> represents September 23, 1995.</p>
<p>But consider what happens when trying to parse it - first the old way, and then the Java 8+ way:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Date dd = new SimpleDateFormat(patt).parse(date);
System.out.println(dd);

DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(patt);
LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse(date, formatter);
System.out.println(d);
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Sat Sep 23 00:00:00 EDT 1995
Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.time.format.DateTimeParseException: Text '2391995' could not be parsed at index 7
    ...
</code></pre>
<p>As we can see, the old way, using <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> works as intended. But when using <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> and <code>LocalDate.parse</code>, an error occurs. Why is this?</p>

Answers:
```java
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.time.format.DateTimeParseException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String date = "2391995";
        String patt = "ddMyyyy";

        try {
            DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(patt);
            LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse(date, formatter);
            System.out.println(d);
        } catch (DateTimeParseException e) {
            System.out.println("Exception in thread 'main' java.time.format.DateTimeParseException: Text '" + date + "' could not be parsed at index " + e.getMessage().split("at index ")[1]);
        }
    }
}
```

Questions:
Title: Get past plus future date and get all dates in said range.
Body: <p>I am using Howard Hinnant's awesome <a href="https://howardhinnant.github.io/date/date.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">date</a> library to do some date calculations.</p>
<p>What I want to do is get Monday's date wherever I am and then get Sunday's date wherever I am. Then I want to get all the dates in between said Monday and Sunday. I don't need time info, just the dates</p>
<p>With this <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/56505034/how-to-find-the-date-of-the-previous-monday-in-c">answer</a>, I am able to get Monday's date as per Howard Hinnant's answer.</p>
<p>What I am struggling with is going <em>forward</em> to calculate Sunday's and thus I'm not sure how to get all the dates between the ranges (Monday - Sunday)</p>
<p>This what I have tried thus far:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

#include &lt;date/date.h&gt;

int main()
{
    auto currentDateYMD = date::year_month_day{ date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now()) };
    auto todays_date = date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now());
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Current Date: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, currentDateYMD) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;

    date::year_month_day mondayDate = todays_date - (date::weekday{ todays_date } - date::Monday);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Monday: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, mondayDate) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;

    todays_date = date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now());

    todays_date += date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() }; // should go forward to Sunday's date
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sunday: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, todays_date) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;; // prints today's date

    // calculate range
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i &lt; /*date::Sunday.c_encoding()*/ 7; i++) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Future Date: &quot; &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, ++todays_date) &lt;&lt; &quot; \n&quot;;
    }

    std::cin.get();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which outputs this:</p>
<pre><code>Current Date: 2023-09-04
Monday: 2023-09-04
Sunday: 2023-09-04
Future Date: 2023-09-05
Future Date: 2023-09-06
Future Date: 2023-09-07
Future Date: 2023-09-08
Future Date: 2023-09-09
Future Date: 2023-09-10
Future Date: 2023-09-11
</code></pre>
<p>I am not sure what I am missing nor am I sure how to &quot;traverse the range&quot; to get dates</p>
<p>I am using C++17 thus I am using the <code>date</code> library and not <code>std::chrono</code>.</p>

Answers:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <date/date.h>

int main()
{
    auto currentDate = date::floor<date::days>(std::chrono::system_clock::now());
    std::cout << "Current Date: " << date::format("%F", currentDate) << std::endl;

    date::year_month_day mondayDate = currentDate - (date::weekday(currentDate) - date::Monday);
    std::cout << "Monday: " << date::format("%F", mondayDate) << std::endl;

    date::year_month_day sundayDate = currentDate + date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() };
    std::cout << "Sunday: " << date::format("%F", sundayDate) << std::endl;

    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < date::Sunday.c_encoding(); i++) {
        date::year_month_day nextDate = sundayDate + date::days{ date::Sunday.c_encoding() };
        std::cout << "Future Date: " << date::format("%F", nextDate) << std::endl;
    }

    std::cin.get();
    return 0;
}
```

