        &lt;SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition=&quot;$([MSBuild]::GetTargetPlatformIdentifier('$(TargetFramework)')) == 'windows'&quot;&gt;10.0.17763.0&lt;/SupportedOSPlatformVersion&gt;
        &lt;TargetPlatformMinVersion Condition=&quot;$([MSBuild]::GetTargetPlatformIdentifier('$(TargetFramework)')) == 'windows'&quot;&gt;10.0.17763.0&lt;/TargetPlatformMinVersion&gt;
        &lt;SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition=&quot;$([MSBuild]::GetTargetPlatformIdentifier('$(TargetFramework)')) == 'tizen'&quot;&gt;6.5&lt;/SupportedOSPlatformVersion&gt;
    &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
        &lt;!-- App Icon --&gt;
        &lt;MauiIcon Include=&quot;Resources\AppIcon\appicon.svg&quot; ForegroundFile=&quot;Resources\AppIcon\appiconfg.svg&quot; Color=&quot;#512BD4&quot; /&gt;

        &lt;!-- Splash Screen --&gt;
        &lt;MauiSplashScreen Include=&quot;Resources\Splash\splash.svg&quot; Color=&quot;#512BD4&quot; BaseSize=&quot;128,128&quot; /&gt;

        &lt;!-- Images --&gt;
        &lt;MauiImage Include=&quot;Resources\Images\*&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;MauiImage Update=&quot;Resources\Images\dotnet_bot.svg&quot; BaseSize=&quot;168,208&quot; /&gt;

        &lt;!-- Custom Fonts --&gt;
        &lt;MauiFont Include=&quot;Resources\Fonts\*&quot; /&gt;

        &lt;!-- Raw Assets (also remove the &quot;Resources\Raw&quot; prefix) --&gt;
        &lt;MauiAsset Include=&quot;Resources\Raw\**&quot; LogicalName=&quot;%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

    &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
        &lt;PackageReference Include=&quot;Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Debug&quot; Version=&quot;7.0.0&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Project <strong>ClassLibrary1.csproj</strong> (project):</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Project Sdk=&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk&quot;&gt;

  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net7.0-windows&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;
    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;
    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;
  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;

  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include=&quot;FlaUI.Core&quot; Version=&quot;4.0.0&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;PackageReference Include=&quot;FlaUI.UIA3&quot; Version=&quot;4.0.0&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;

&lt;/Project&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Currently if:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/orBxD.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/orBxD.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>I get following errors:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/VSg97.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/VSg97.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>My question is How to reference ClassLibrary1.csproj in MauiApp1.csproj in NET MAUI? Is there some way to get this setup working?</p>

Title: Style based on default style MAUI.
Body: <p>I have default style for all <code>Label</code></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Style TargetType=&quot;Label&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Setter ...
&lt;/Style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then I have style with <code>x:Key</code> for <code>Label</code></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Style x:Key=&quot;notForAll&quot; TargetType=&quot;Label&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Setter ...
&lt;/Style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>So, if i do this</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Label Style=&quot;{StaticResource notForAll}&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This label will not include default label style, only <code>notForAll</code> style.</p>
<p><strong>How can I base <code>notForAll</code> style on <code>default</code> label style</strong>? I can`t put default style into <code>BasedOn</code></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Style x:Key=&quot;notForAll&quot; TargetType=&quot;Label&quot; BasedOn=&quot;{StaticResource ????????????}&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Setter ...
&lt;/Style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I found <strong>solution for WPF</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Style x:Key=&quot;NamedStyle&quot; TargetType=&quot;Label&quot; BasedOn=&quot;{StaticResource {x:Type Label}}&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Setter ...
&lt;/Style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>But it <strong>doesn't work for MAUI</strong>.</p>

Answers:
```csharp
using MauiApp1.Models;
using MauiApp1.Services;

public partial class MainPage : ContentPage
{
    private readonly ILabelService _lblService;

    public MainPage(ILabelService lblService)
    {
        _lblService = lblService;
        InitializeComponent();
    }

    private async void LblName_Loaded(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        var text = _lblService.GetLblNameText();
        var textSpan = new TextSpan(text, 0, text.Length, System.Text.StringBuilder.Empty, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8);
        var label = new Label
        {
            Content = textSpan,
            X = 20,
            Y = 20,
            WidthRequest = 200,
            FontSize = 20,
            LineBreakMode = LineBreakMode.WordWrap,
            TextColor = Color.White,
            HorizontalOptions = HorizontalOptions.None
        };

        ContentLayout.SetContent(label, true);
    }
}
```

Questions:
Title: What&#39;s the Raku equivalent of the super keyword as used in JavaScript and Python?.
Body: <p>Whenever you extend a class in JavaScript or Python, the derived class must use the <code>super</code> keyword in order to set attributes and/or invoke methods and constructor in the base class. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>class Rectangle {
    constructor(length, width) {
        this.name = &quot;Rectangle&quot;;
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    shoutArea() {
        console.log(
            `I AM A ${this.name.toUpperCase()} AND MY AREA IS ${this.length * this.width}`
        );
    }

    rectHello() {
        return &quot;Rectanglish: hello&quot;;
    }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    constructor(length) {
        super(length, length);
        this.name = &quot;Square&quot;
    }

    squaHello() {
        const h = super.rectHello();
        return &quot;Squarish:&quot; + h.split(':')[1];
    }
}

const rect = new Rectangle(6, 4);
rect.shoutArea(); //=&gt; I AM A RECTANGLE AND MY AREA IS 24

const squa = new Square(5);
squa.shoutArea(); //=&gt; I AM A SQUARE AND MY AREA IS 25

console.log(squa.squaHello()); //=&gt; Squarish: hello
</code></pre>

Answers:
None
class Rectangle {
    constructor(length, width) {
        this.name = "Rectangle";
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    shoutArea() {
        console.log(`I AM A ${this.name.toUpperCase()} AND MY AREA IS ${this.length * this.width}`);
    }

    rectHello() {
        return "Rectanglish: hello";
    }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    constructor(length) {
        super(length, length);
        this.name = "Square";
    }

    squaHello() {
        const h = super.rectHello();
        return "Squarish:" + h.split(':')[1];
    }
}

const rect = new Rectangle(6, 4);
rect.shoutArea(); // I AM A RECTANGLE AND MY AREA IS 24

const squa = new Square(5);
squa.shoutArea(); // I AM A SQUARE AND MY AREA IS 25

console.log(squa.squaHello()); // Squarish: hello

Questions:
Title: Memory leak using unique pointer and polymorphism.
Body: <p>I know that <code>std::unique_ptr</code> doesn't guarantee memory safety and especially it is true in the case of circular dependency. However, in my case, I can't see it. <code>Child</code> contain <code>Parent</code>(or <code>SecondChild</code> in case of polymorphism), but neither of them contains <code>Child</code>.</p>
<p>Valgrind reports <code>4 bytes</code> lost so I suppose that <code>SecondChild</code> was not destroyed. My code really relies on polymorphism so I hope to get advice on refactoring that won't change <code>Parent*</code> in <code>Child</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
using namespace std;

struct Parent
{
    Parent() {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Expr created&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    ~Parent() {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Expr destroyed&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
};

struct Child : public Parent 
{
    std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; content;
};

struct SecondChild : public Parent 
{
    int val;
};

std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; foo()
{
    auto test = make_unique&lt;Child&gt;();
    auto content_in_child = make_unique&lt;SecondChild&gt;();
    content_in_child-&gt;val = 4;
    test-&gt;content = std::move(content_in_child);
    return test;
}

int main()
{
    std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; high = foo();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Answers:
None
```cpp
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
using namespace std;

// Define a parent class with a destructor to print creation and destruction messages
struct Parent
{
    Parent() {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Expr created&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    ~Parent() {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Expr destroyed&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    }
};

// Define a child class that inherits from the parent class
struct Child : public Parent 
{
    // Use a unique pointer to hold a pointer to a parent object
    std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; content;
};

// Define a second child class that also inherits from the parent class
struct SecondChild : public Parent 
{
    int val; // Note: This is not a child object, it's a separate entity
};

// Define a function that returns a unique pointer to a parent object
std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; foo()
{
    // Create a child object using make_unique
    auto test = make_unique&lt;Child&gt;();
    // Create a second child object
    auto content_in_child = make_unique&lt;SecondChild&gt;();
    // Set the value of the second child object
    content_in_child-&gt;val = 4;
    // Set the content pointer of the child object to the second child object
    test-&gt;content = std::move(content_in_child);
    return test;
}

int main()
{
    // Create a unique pointer to a parent object
    std::unique_ptr&lt;Parent&gt; high = foo();
    return 0;
}
```

