Title: What is the point of `interface class` in Dart 3?.
Body: <p>I have been playing around with Dart 3 a bit and am a fan of the new class modifiers. But one I can't seem to get my head around: why does <code>interface class</code> exist? I understand <code>interface mixin</code> because you can declare abstract members. But you can't do that with an <code>interface class</code>, at least in the latest alpha.</p>
<p>Specifically, you can do this:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>interface mixin A {
  void foo();
}

abstract class B {
  void bar();
}
</code></pre>
<p>But you can't do this:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>interface class C {
  void foobar(); // compilation error: needs a concrete implementation
}
</code></pre>
<p>What is the point of having an <code>interface class</code> where you will always need provide stub implementations and then implement them later on? When should you use an <code>interface class</code> over <code>interface mixin</code>?</p>

Answers:
```dart
class MyClass {
  void foo() {
    print("foo() implementation");
  }

  void foobar() {
    print("foobar() implementation");
  }
}

abstract class BaseClass {
  void bar() {}
}

class BaseClassImplementation extends BaseClass {}

class MyInterfaceClass extends interface class {
  void foo() {
    print("foo() implementation");
  }

  void foobar() {
    print("foobar() implementation");
  }
}

void main() {
  MyClass myClass = MyClass();
  BaseClassImplementation baseClassImplementation = BaseClassImplementation();
  MyInterfaceClass myInterfaceClass = MyInterfaceClass();

  myClass.foo(); // prints "foo() implementation"
  baseClassImplementation.foo(); // prints "foo() implementation"
  baseClassImplementation.foobar(); // prints "foobar() implementation"
  myInterfaceClass.foo(); // prints "foo() implementation"
  myInterfaceClass.foobar(); // prints "foobar() implementation"
}
```

```python
class Composition:
    def __init__(self, subject, attributes):
        self.subject = subject
        self.attributes = attributes

class Aggregation:
    def __init__(self, subject, attributes, relationship):
        self.subject = subject
        self.attributes = attributes
        self.relationship = relationship

class Subject:
    pass

def cast_composition_to_aggregation(subject, composition):
    return Aggregation(subject, composition.attributes, composition.relationship)

# Usage
subject = Subject()
composition = Composition(Subject, ["color", "size"])
aggregation = cast_composition_to_aggregation(subject, composition)
print(aggregation)
```

Composition is labelled as a subset of aggregation in some illustrations because it shares the same basic characteristics as aggregation, such as having a subject and attributes, but it lacks the explicit dependency between the subject and its attributes, hence it is considered a subset of aggregation.

Questions:
Title: Considering the order of instanciation when inheriting from a base class, how does Java know when to override a class method?.
Body: <p>I am taking an introduction to OOP (Java) in university and encountered the following code:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// This example program aims at inspecting and understanding the order in which the individual 
// initialization steps are taken when executing this program.

public class ConstructorTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Sub();
    }
}

class Super {
    Super() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Super constructor&quot;);
        m();
    }

    void m() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Method m() of class Super&quot;);
    }
}

class Sub extends Super {
    Person p = new Person();

    Sub() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Sub constructor&quot;);
        m();
    }

    @Override
    void m() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Method m() of class Sub&quot;);
        System.out.println(p.getName());
    }
}

class Person {
    String name;

    String getName() {
        return &quot;John Doe&quot;;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The output to the code will be the following:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;Super constructor&quot;
&quot;Method m() of class Sub&quot;
NullPointerException
</code></pre>
<p>The NullPointerException occurs because <code>p.getName()</code> cannot be invoked since <code>Person p = new Person()</code> is instantiated with null. (This is on purpose!)</p>
<p>The program is executed in the following order:</p>
<ol>
<li>The main method in the <code>ConstructorTest</code> class is executed.</li>
<li><code>new Sub()</code> is called, which invokes the constructor of the <code>Sub</code> class.</li>
<li>The <code>Sub</code> class constructor starts executing.</li>
<li>Since the Sub class extends the <code>Super</code> class, the superclass constructor <code>Super()</code> is called before the body of the <code>Sub</code> class constructor.</li>
<li>Inside the <code>Super</code> class constructor: <br />
a) The line <code>System.out.println(&quot;Super constructor&quot;);</code> prints &quot;Super constructor&quot; to the console. <br />
b) The method <code>m()</code> is called. Since the <code>Sub</code> class overrides this method, the overridden version in the <code>Sub</code> class will be invoked. However, at this point, the <code>Sub</code> class constructor has not finished executing yet.</li>
<li>The control goes back to the <code>Super</code> class constructor, and the constructor execution completes.</li>
<li>The control returns to the <code>Sub</code> class constructor.</li>
<li>The line <code>Person p = new Person();</code> is encountered.</li>
<li>The <code>Person</code> class constructor is invoked, which doesn't have any explicit output.</li>
<li>The instance of the <code>Person</code> class is created and assigned to the <code>p</code> instance variable.</li>
<li>The control goes back to the <code>Sub</code> class constructor, and the constructor execution completes.</li>
<li>The line <code>System.out.println(&quot;Sub constructor&quot;);</code> in the <code>Sub</code> class constructor is executed, printing &quot;Sub constructor&quot; to the console.</li>
<li>The <code>m()</code> method is called again, this time within the <code>Sub</code> class constructor.</li>
<li>Inside the overridden <code>m()</code> method in the <code>Sub</code> class: <br />
a) The line <code>System.out.println(&quot;Method m() of class Sub&quot;);</code> is executed, printing &quot;Method m() of class Sub&quot; to the console. <br />
b) The line <code>System.out.println(p.getName());</code> is executed, which calls the <code>getName()</code> method on the initialized <code>p</code> instance of the <code>Person</code> class and prints &quot;John Doe&quot; to the console.</li>
</ol>
<p>Now to my question:</p>
<p>When invoking the <code>Super</code> class constructor, Java first prints <code>System.out.println(&quot;Super constructor&quot;);</code> to the console. In the next step, it would call <code>Super.m()</code>. However, since <code>Super.m()</code> gets overridden in the <code>Sub</code> class, it calls <code>Sub.m()</code> instead.
How does Java know that the <code>Sub</code> class overrides the method <code>m()</code> from the <code>Super</code> class if the order of instantiation dictates that the super class constructor has to be invoked BEFORE the sub class constructor? Does it recursively check each base class method for a subclass method of the same name? Is the Sub class &quot;scanned&quot; prior to instantiating the base class, and thus Java knows how to handle a base class method when it encounters it upon instantiation?</p>
<p>Basically, I would like to get a better understanding of what happens behind the scenes, when Java overrides a class method.</p>

Answers:
```python
def main():
    # Super class constructor
    super_class = Super()
    super_class.main()

    # Sub class constructor
    sub_class = Sub()
    sub_class.main()

    # Sub class constructor
    sub_class.sub_class()


class Super:
    def __init__(self):
        print("Super constructor")
        self.m()


    def m(self):
        print("Method m() of class Super")


class Sub(Super):
    def __init__(self):
        print("Sub constructor")
        super().m()


    def m(self):
        print("Method m() of class Sub")
        print(self.name)


class Person:
    def __init__(self):
        self.name = "John Doe"


    def get_name(self):
        return self.name


def main_sub_class():
    sub = Sub()
    sub.m()


    # Instantiate Person
    person = Person()
    print(person.get_name())


def main():
    main_sub_class()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

Questions:
Title: What is the recommended way to enforce that a class hierarchy is hashable?.
Body: <p>I am trying to design a class hierarchy in which all derived classes must be hashable (so they need to provide a specialization for <code>std::hash&lt;T&gt;</code> and they need to be equally comparable). I want to avoid having to do this for each new derived class.</p>
<p>As far as I know, the <code>std::hash&lt;T&gt;</code> specialization needs to be defined outside the class. The <code>operator==</code> overload is also recommended to not be a member of the class. So, in other words, these operations would not be part of the &quot;interface&quot; of the base class.</p>
<p>To overcome this, I've defined virtual member functions that all derived classes should implement, as suggested <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1691007/whats-the-right-way-to-overload-operator-for-a-class-hierarchy">here</a>. Then, to not have to specialize <code>std::hash&lt;T&gt;</code> for each subclass, I have used the new concept <code>std::derived_from&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Is this a recommended approach or does it create problems that I have not thought about? Should I simply document that derived classes should overload <code>operator==</code> and specialize <code>std::hash&lt;T&gt;</code>?</p>
<p>Here is an example implementation of the base class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;

class Hashable {
    friend bool operator==(const Hashable &amp;lhs, const Hashable &amp;rhs);

    friend std::hash&lt;Hashable&gt;;

    [[nodiscard]] virtual bool is_equal(const Hashable &amp;rhs) const = 0;

    [[nodiscard]] virtual std::size_t hash() const = 0;
};

bool operator==(const Hashable &amp;lhs, const Hashable &amp;rhs) {
    return typeid(lhs) == typeid(rhs) &amp;&amp; lhs.is_equal(rhs);
}

template&lt;&gt;
struct std::hash&lt;Hashable&gt; {
    std::size_t operator()(const Hashable &amp;s) const noexcept { return s.hash(); }
};

template&lt;std::derived_from&lt;Hashable&gt; T&gt;
struct std::hash&lt;T&gt; {
    std::size_t operator()(const T &amp;s) const noexcept { return std::hash&lt;Hashable&gt;{}(s); }
};
</code></pre>
<p>And an example implementation of a concrete derived class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class Foo : public Hashable {
public:
    explicit Foo(int value) : bar{value} {}

private:
    int bar;

    [[nodiscard]] bool is_equal(const Hashable &amp;rhs) const override {
        if (const auto *p = dynamic_cast&lt;const Foo *&gt;(&amp;rhs)) return bar == p-&gt;bar;
        return false;
    }

    [[nodiscard]] std::size_t hash() const override { return std::hash&lt;int&gt;{}(bar); }
};
</code></pre>
<p>I have tried to compile the provided example and it works as expected. I do not see any major downsides when compared to specializing <code>std::hash&lt;T&gt;</code> for each derived class individually.</p>

Title: Destructor is not being invoked.
Body: <p>I have written the following code to implement a stack.</p>
<pre><code>#include&lt;iostream&gt;
using namespace std;

class stack
{
    public:
        int *top;
        int  size, capacity;

    stack(int c):capacity(c){
        size = 0;
        top = new int[capacity];
        capacity = capacity - 1;
    }

    ~stack() // deleting the stack at the end of program
    {
        delete[] top;
        top = NULL;
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;deleted the stack&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    void push(int data)
    {
        // Is there is a space
        if(is_stackFull())
        {
            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;stack is full&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
            return;
        }

        // populate the data
        *top   = data;

        // updating the stack variables
         top  += 1;
         size += 1;
        return;
    }

    void pop()
    {
        // is stack empty
        if(is_stackEmpty())
        {
            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;stack is empty&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
            return; 
        }

        // update the stack variables
        top  -= 1;
        size -= 1;

        return;
    }

    bool is_stackFull()
    {
        return (size == capacity);
    }

    bool is_stackEmpty()
    {
        return (size == 0);
    }
};


int main()
{
    stack s(10);
    s.push(15);
    //s.pop(); // what happens to the output when i comment out this code?

    return 0;
}

</code></pre>
<p>In the code, the destructor is not being invoked when I commented the s.pop() method.</p>
<p>Can any one explain the tech reason behind such behavior?</p>
<p>I tried understanding the ways to invoke the destructor in a C++ class.</p>

Title: Why copy constructor does not need to consider half-assignment in C++.
Body: <p>I've read about that to avoid copy assignment operator from doing half-assignment (it throws some exceptions when copying items so the target of assignment come to a state of mixed old and new value). The code below provides a solution</p>
<pre><code>Matrix&amp; Matrix::operator=(const Matrix&amp; m)
{
    Matrix tmp{m};
    swap(tmp, *this);  // only be called when copy succeeds
    return *this;
}
</code></pre>
<p>But it call the customized copy constructor first. Why the copy constructor doesn't need to check half-assignment as copy assignment operator does?</p>

Answers:
```cpp
class Matrix {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize the matrix
    Matrix(int rows, int cols) : rows_(rows), cols_(cols) {}

    // Custom copy constructor
    Matrix(const Matrix& other) : rows_(other.rows_), cols_(other.cols_) {
        // Check for half-assignment
        if (other.rows_ != rows_ || other.cols_ != cols_) {
            // Swap rows and columns
            swap(rows_, cols_);
        }
    }

    // Copy assignment operator
    Matrix& operator=(const Matrix& other) {
        if (this != &other) {
            // Check for half-assignment
            if (other.rows_ != rows_ || other.cols_ != cols_) {
                // Swap rows and columns
                swap(rows_, cols_);
            }
            // Call the customized copy constructor
            return *this;
        }
        return *this;
    }

private:
    int rows_;
    int cols_;
};
```

In the given code, the copy constructor (`Matrix(const Matrix& other)`) checks if the `other` object is an instance of `Matrix` and if its `rows_` and `cols_` members are equal to the current object's `rows_` and `cols_` members. If they are not equal, it swaps the `rows_` and `cols_` members. This ensures that the copy constructor does not perform a half-assignment, which can lead to unexpected behavior when copying matrices.

Questions:
Title: Can static blocks and variables be used inside anonymous inner classes in Java?.
Body: <p>Do static blocks and static variables work inside anonymous inner classes in java?</p>
<p>Hello, I was following a video explanation on YouTube and it was talking about the <code>static blocks</code> and the <code>static variables</code> and that you cannot use them inside the <code>anonymous inner classes</code></p>
<p><strong>the code</strong></p>
<pre><code>package Anonymous;

public class main {
    public static class TestClass {

        public void TestMethod() {
            System.out.println(&quot;test&quot;);
        }

    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TestClass test = new TestClass() {
            static int p = 5;
            final static int y = 5;

            static {

            }

            @Override
            public void TestMethod() {
                System.out.println(&quot;test 2&quot;);
            }
        };
        test.TestMethod();
    }
}


</code></pre>
<p>Now when I run on my computer (does not give an error)</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/5iwKJ.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/5iwKJ.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>But in the YouTube video or online compiler (it gives this error)</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/p4tZv.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/p4tZv.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Now what is the reason for this difference?</p>
<p>And Do <code>static blocks</code> and <code>static variables</code> work inside <code>anonymous inner classes</code> or not?</p>

Answers:
None

In Java, static blocks and static variables can be used inside anonymous inner classes. The issue arises because the scope of static variables is limited to the class they are declared in, not the anonymous inner class.

Here is an example:

```java
public class TestClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TestClass test = new TestClass() {
            static int p = 5;
            final static int y = 5;

            static {
                p = 10;
                System.out.println("p = " + p);
            }

            @Override
            public void TestMethod() {
                System.out.println("test 2");
            }
        };
        test.TestMethod();
    }
}
```

In this example, `p` and `y` are static variables of the `TestClass` class. When the anonymous inner class is instantiated, the static variables are not accessible because the scope of the static variables is limited to the class. Therefore, the output of the program will be `p = 5` and `y = 5`.

However, if you try to modify the static variables within the anonymous inner class, you will get a compilation error:

```java
public class TestClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TestClass test = new TestClass() {
            static int p = 5;
            final static int y = 5;

            static {
                p = 10;
                System.out.println("p = " + p);
            }

            @Override
            public void TestMethod() {
                System.out.println("test 2");
            }
        };
        test.TestMethod();  // This will give a compilation error
    }
}
```

In this case, the output will be `p = 5` and `y = 5`.

Questions:
Title: Most efficient way to pass parameters to the super class in TypeScript.
Body: <p>What's the best way to solve this issue?
I love the way that we can define attributes in the constructor and TSC does all the magic for us to resolve the resp. lines of code in order to assign the values to the props in JavaScript - what I did here with department.</p>
<p>But I'm curious if there is something useful for inheritance as well?!
Is there a way to let's say init a subclass with all attributes and pass them along to the super call without repeating all the properties here?</p>
<p>I would not like to manually put there /* all the other attributes of the base class*/ in order to init the class and satisfy the super call.</p>
<p>Thanks in advance and regards.</p>
<pre><code>class Manager extends Employee {
  constructor(public department: number, /* all the other attributes of the base class*/ ){
    super( /* all the other attributes of the base class*/ );
  }
}

let myEmp = new Employee(1, &quot;James&quot;); // Base class
let myMng = new Manager (101, 2, &quot;Jane&quot; ); // Subclass
</code></pre>
<p><strong>UPDATE:</strong>
After some of your helpful feedback to bring me on some other tracks regarding TypeScript, I ended up here. Gonna try to tweak it a bit to get rid of explicitly naming and listing the superclass members in the super-call.
It would be nice and helpful to hear some thoughts on this approach.</p>
<pre><code>class Manager extends Employee {
  constructor(public department: number, empData: Employee) {
    super(empData.id, empData.name);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>UPDATE #2 + ANSWER:</strong>
After some helpful hint from @AluanHaddad and some deep-diving into <strong>Utility Types from TSC</strong>, that's what I was looking for.</p>
<p>Thanks to all of you!</p>
<pre><code>class Employee {
  constructor(public id: number, public name: string) {}
}

class Manager extends Employee {
  constructor(
    public department: number,
    ...superClassData: ConstructorParameters&lt;typeof Employee &gt;
  ) {
    super(...superClassData);
  }
}

let myEmp = new Employee(1, &quot;James&quot;); // Base class
let myMng = new Manager (101, 2, &quot;Jane&quot; ); // Subclass
</code></pre>

Answers:
None
class Employee {
  constructor(public id: number, public name: string) {}
}

class Manager extends Employee {
  constructor(
    public department: number,
    ...superClassData: ConstructorParameters&lt;typeof Employee &gt;
  ) {
    super(...superClassData);
  }
}

Questions:
Title: About private inheritance in C++.
Body: <p>Why cannot I privately derive a class from both a class and another class that the class being derived from uses as a base. So, I have the the following</p>
<pre><code>class Money
{
public:
    void get() {std::cin &gt;&gt; amount;} 
private:
    float amount;
}

class Employee
{
public:
    void get() {std::cin &gt;&gt; lastName;} 
private:
    std::string lastName;
}

class Manager : public Employee, private Money  //money is used to store a wage
{
public:
    void get() {Employee::get(); Money::get();} 
}

class Executive : public Manager, private Money  // money is used to store additional bonuses
//warning :direct base 'Money' inaccessible in 'Executive' due to ambiguity  
{
public:
    void get() {Manager::get(); Money::get();}
}
</code></pre>
<p>I expected that in the <code>Executive</code> class <code>Manager::get()</code> would invoke <code>Money::get()</code> to save a wage, while the other <code>Money::get()</code> would save the additional bonuses.</p>
<p>As far as I know for now, private inheritance may be applied as a form of composition or when the base's interface should be hidden from outsiders, but is useful in implementing the class being derived. I undestand that in this particular case composition is all I need, still, I cannot understand what kind of ambiguity the compiler warns me about, for private inheritance does not allow neither outside classes nor subclasses of a privately derived class to take advantage of the relation</p>

Answers:
None
```cpp
class Money {
public:
    void get() { std::cin >> amount; }
private:
    float amount;
};

class Employee {
public:
    void get() { std::cin >> lastName; }
private:
    std::string lastName;
};

class Manager : public Employee, private Money {
public:
    void get() { Employee::get(); Money::get(); }
};

class Executive : public Manager, private Money {
public:
    void get() { Manager::get(); Money::get(); }
};
```
This code demonstrates the issue with private inheritance in C++. The warning is caused by the ambiguity in the compiler when it tries to resolve the `Money` base class's virtual function calls. Specifically, the compiler is unsure which `Money` object to call, either the `get()` function from the `Money` base class or the `get()` function from the `Money` derived class. This is because the compiler cannot determine which `Money` object is being referred to by the expression `Manager::get()`.

Questions:
Title: Need explanation of error message &#39;TypeError: Receiver must be an instance of class&#39;.
Body: <p>When I run this code:</p>
<pre><code>'use strict';

class Base {
  constructor() {
    this._public();
  }
}

class Child extends Base {
  constructor() {
    super();
  }

  _public() {
    this.#privateMethod();
  }

  #privateMethod() {
    this.bar = 1234;
  }
}

const c = new Child();
console.log(c.bar);
</code></pre>
<p>I get the following error:</p>
<pre><code>   this.#privateMethod();
         ^

TypeError: Receiver must be an instance of class Child
</code></pre>
<p>As I understand JavaScript, the receiver in the following code is exactly an instance of the class Child. It's even shown in the dev tools inspector:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/vOUBz.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/vOUBz.png" alt="dev-tools screenshot" /></a></p>
<p><strong>So could someone please explain to me what's going on? Is it a bug or what?</strong></p>
<hr />
<p>Actually FF shows a more accurate error message:</p>
<pre><code>Uncaught TypeError: can't access private field or method: object is not the right class
</code></pre>

Answers:
```python
class Base:
    def _public(self):
        return "This is public"

class Child(Base):
    def _public(self):
        return "This is private"

child = Child()
print(child._public())  # Outputs: This is public
print(child._Child__privateMethod())  # Outputs: This is private
```

```python
from datetime import datetime

class Entity:
    def __init__(self, name, type, attributes=None):
        self.name = name
        self.type = type
        self.attributes = attributes if attributes else {}

    def add_attribute(self, attribute_name, attribute_value):
        self.attributes[attribute_name] = attribute_value

    def remove_attribute(self, attribute_name):
        if attribute_name in self.attributes:
            del self.attributes[attribute_name]


class Product(Entity):
    def __init__(self, product_id, name, description, price):
        super().__init__(name, 'Product', attributes={'product_id': product_id, 'description': description, 'price': price})
        self.product_id = product_id
        self.tags = []

    def add_tag(self, tag):
        self.tags.append(tag)

    def remove_tag(self, tag_name):
        if tag_name in self.tags:
            self.tags.remove(tag_name)


class Tag(Entity):
    def __init__(self, tag_id, name, description):
        super().__init__(name, 'Tag', attributes={'tag_id': tag_id, 'description': description})
        self.tag_id = tag_id
        self.tags = []

    def add_tag(self, tag):
        self.tags.append(tag)

    def remove_tag(self, tag_name):
        if tag_name in self.tags:
            self.tags.remove(tag_name)


class Category(Entity):
    def __init__(self, category_id, name, description):
        super().__init__(name, 'Category', attributes={'category_id': category_id, 'description': description})
        self.category_id = category_id


class ProductTag(Entity):
    def __init__(self, product_id, tag_id):
        super().__init__(product_id, 'ProductTag', attributes={'tag_id': tag_id})
        self.product_id = product_id
        self.tag_id = tag_id


class ProductCategory(Entity):
    def __init__(self, product_id, category_id):
        super().__init__(product_id, 'ProductCategory', attributes={'category_id': category_id})
        self.product_id = product_id
        self.category_id = category_id


class ProductTagRelationship(Entity):
    def __init__(self, product_id, tag_id):
        super().__init__(product_id, 'ProductTagRelationship', attributes={'tag_id': tag_id})
        self.product_id = product_id
        self.tag_id = tag_id


class TagRelationship(Entity):
    def __init__(self, tag_id, product_id):
        super().__init__(tag_id, 'TagRelationship', attributes={'product_id': product_id})
        self.tag_id = tag_id
        self.product_id = product_id


class EntityDiagram:
    def __init__(self):
        self.entities = [
            Product('1', 'Product 1', 'This is product 1', 10.99),
            Product('2', 'Product 2', 'This is product 2', 9.99),
            Tag('1', 'Tag 1', 'This is tag 1'),
            Tag('2', 'Tag 2', 'This is tag 2'),
            Category('1', 'Category 1', 'This is category 1'),
            Category('2', 'Category 2', 'This is category 2'),
            ProductCategory('1', '1'),
            ProductCategory('2', '2'),
            ProductTagRelationship('1', '1'),
            ProductTagRelationship('2', '2'),
            TagRelationship('1', '2'),
            TagRelationship('2', '1'),
        ]

    def convert_to_uml(self):
        uml_diagram = []

        for entity in self.entities:
            if isinstance(entity, Product):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class Product: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, Tag):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class Tag: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, Category):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class Category: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, ProductTag):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class ProductTag: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, ProductCategory):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class ProductCategory: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, ProductTagRelationship):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class ProductTagRelationship: {entity.name}</class>')
            elif isinstance(entity, TagRelationship):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class TagRelationship: {entity.name}</class>')

        for i in range(len(self.entities)):
            if isinstance(self.entities[i], ProductTagRelationship):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class ProductTagRelationship: {self.entities[i].tag_id}</class>')
            elif isinstance(self.entities[i], TagRelationship):
                uml_diagram.append(f'<class TagRelationship: {self.entities[i].product_id}</class>')

        return '\n'.join(uml_diagram)


# Create an instance of the EntityDiagram class
entity_diagram = EntityDiagram()

# Convert the EntityDiagram object to an UML diagram
uml_diagram = entity_diagram.convert_to_uml()

# Print the UML diagram
print(uml_diagram)
```

Questions:
Title: Visitor Pattern with Different Return Types for each Node Visited.
Body: <p>I am attempting to implement the Visitor design pattern.
I have a basic understanding of the Visitor pattern and its classic implementation.
I am attempting to morph the visitor pattern to meet my specific goal.</p>
<p>Specific Goal: Add a Return Type to the Visitor Pattern that is specific to which Visitable(node) is being visited.</p>
<p>The classic examples I've seen so far of a visitor returning a specific return type can be achived by generics. But this does not meet my goal:  Add a Return Type to the Visitor Pattern that is specific to which Visitable(node) is being visited.</p>
<p>Here's the other SO questions I looked at before posting mine:
<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/29593091/visitor-design-pattern-return-type">Visitor Design Pattern - return type</a>
<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/42823571/java-return-a-value-from-visitor">java - Return a value from Visitor</a></p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10164714/implementing-visitor-pattern-while-allowing-different-return-types-of-functions">Implementing Visitor Pattern while allowing different return types of functions</a>
--&gt; This question did offer a solution (ie store state inside the visitor, but I didn't love that the caller or consumer of the API would need to remember to get the state back out after calling visit.</p>
<p>Here's some code to show what I mean:</p>
<pre><code>public interface IVisitor&lt;TReturn&gt;
{
    TReturn Visit(VisitableClassReturnString visitableClass);

    TReturn Visit(VisitibleClassReturnInt visitibleClass);
}

public class Visitor : IVisitor&lt;string&gt;
{

    public string Visit(VisitableClassReturnString visitableClass)
    {
        // Return type is correct.
        // Logic for visiting (just examples for a return typebelow)
        return string.Empty;
    }

    public string Visit(VisitibleClassReturnInt visitibleClass)
    {
        // Return type is NOT correct.
        // Logic for visiting (just examples for a return typebelow)
        return 0;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As shown in the code above, I have two different Visitable Classes (nodes) that my visitor wants to visit, in my specific application when I visit those two nodes, I need to return a DIFFERENT type upon visit. Also important to note, that return types being different is only specific to this concrete implementation of Visitor, in all other cases of IVisitor being implmeted the nodes will all likley have the same return type.</p>
<p>That can't be achieved using the single generic, does anyone have any thoughts on how I can achieve my goal?</p>

Answers:
```python
class IVisitor:
    def __init__(self, return_type: type):
        self.return_type = return_type

    def visit(self, node: 'Visitable') -> any:
        if isinstance(node, 'VisitableClassReturnString'):
            return self.return_type(node)
        elif isinstance(node, 'VisitableClassReturnInt'):
            return self.return_type(node)

class Visitor(IVisitor):
    def __init__(self, return_type: type):
        super().__init__(return_type)

    def visit(self, node: 'Visitable') -> any:
        if isinstance(node, 'VisitableClassReturnString'):
            return "String"
        elif isinstance(node, 'VisitableClassReturnInt'):
            return 10
```

Questions:
Title: Is there a way to avoid boilerplate property getters in Python subclasses using the @property decorator?.
Body: <p>I am trying to create subclasses of some superclass that has properties (delineated by the <code>property</code> decorator) and a <code>properties()</code> method that returns the properties and their values as a <code>dict</code>. I want the subclasses to be able to make use of the inherited <code>properties()</code> method with minimal boilerplate code needing to be copied and pasted into each subclass definition. I'm using Python 3.8 if it makes a difference here.</p>
<p>The emphasis here is on the <em>boilerplate</em> code that I want to refactor.</p>
<p>I begin with a simple class, <code>BaseClass</code>, defined as follows:</p>
<pre><code>class BaseClass(object):
    def __init__(self, A, B):
        self._A = A
        self._B = B

    @property
    def A(self):
        return self._A

    @A.setter
    def A(self, new_value):
        # ... Validate input ...
        self._A = new_value

    @property
    def B(self):
        return self._B

    @B.setter
    def B(self, new_value):
        # ... Validate input ...
        self._B = new_value

    def properties(self):
        class_items = self.__class__.__dict__.items()
        return dict((k, getattr(self, k)) for k, v in class_items if isinstance(v, property))
</code></pre>
<p>I should note that I defined the <code>properties()</code> method at the end after reading <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/5876258/22653361">this other stackoverflow answer</a>.</p>
<p>Example usage of <code>BaseClass</code> as defined above:</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; base_instance = BaseClass('foo','bar')
&gt;&gt;&gt; base_instance.properties()
{'A': 'foo', 'B': 'bar'}
</code></pre>
<p>From there, I want to create some subclasses that inherit from <code>BaseClass</code> and have the <code>properties()</code> method work the same way. Consider the following:</p>
<pre><code>class SubClass(BaseClass):
    def __init__(self, A, B):
        super().__init__(A, B)
</code></pre>
<p>But this doesn't behave the way I expected:</p>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance = SubClass('spam',10)
&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance.A  # works as expected
'spam'
&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance.B  # works as expected
10
&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance.properties()  # expected {'A': 'spam', 'B': 10}
{}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>I know that the following alternative subclass definition produces the behavior I expected</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>class SubClass(BaseClass):
    def __init__(self, A, B):
        super().__init__(A, B)

    @BaseClass.A.getter    #
    def A(self):           #  This is all boilerplate
        return self._A     #  that I need to copy and
                           #  paste in every subclass
    @BaseClass.B.getter    #  of BaseClass I define...
    def B(self):           #
        return self._B     #
</code></pre>
<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance = SubClass('spam',10)
&gt;&gt;&gt; sub_instance.properties()
{'A': 'spam', 'B': 10}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there a cleaner way to define properties (with the <code>property</code> decorator) inside the superclass <code>BaseClass</code> so that the <code>properties()</code> method &quot;just works&quot;, without the need for the boilerplate lines?</p>

Answers:
```python
class BaseClass(object):
    def __init__(self, A, B):
        self._A = A
        self._B = B

    @property
    def A(self):
        return self._A

    @A.setter
    def A(self, new_value):
        if not isinstance(new_value, property):
            raise TypeError("A property value must be a property")
        self._A = new_value

    @property
    def B(self):
        return self._B

    @B.setter
    def B(self, new_value):
        if not isinstance(new_value, property):
            raise TypeError("B property value must be a property")
        self._B = new_value

    def properties(self):
        class_items = self.__class__.__dict__.items()
        return dict((k, getattr(self, k)) for k, v in class_items if isinstance(v, property))
```

