Questions:
Title: If statement without curly braces.
Body: <p>been looking at this one problem I came across. The question is what does the following snippet return.</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    int a = 1, b = 2, c = 3, d = 4;
    int x = a;
    if (a &gt; b)
    if (b &lt; c) x = b;
    else x = c;
    return(x);
}
</code></pre>
<p>As I understand this, if statements without a curly brace are allowed and will execute the immediate statement following it. So in the case of the first condition (a &gt; b), if true, then the following line will execute as the statement. And then if the nested if statements' condition (b &lt; c) is also true, then the statement following it will be executed. And the else statement would &quot;belong&quot; to the first if statement. However, putting this into a compiler shows me that the else statement actually belongs to the nested if statement and returns 1.</p>
<p>Could someone explain what I am misunderstanding? Does the else statement, in the case without any curly braces, also belong to the closest if statement?</p>
<p>And yes, I understand this is a poorly written piece of code with the readability out the window. I would not do this in practice but still am curious about the correct way to interpret this program.</p>
<p>I ran the program and saw that it returns 1, which indicates to me that the else statement is actually part of the nested if statement, which contradicts the fact if statements without curly braces execute only the line immediately following it.</p>

Answers:
None
if (a &gt; b):
    if (b &lt; c):
        x = b
    else:
        x = c
print(x)

None
You can achieve this by utilizing the `functools` module's ` wraps` function to dynamically wrap the input function with a different return type. Here's an example of how you can do it:

```python
import functools

def myFun(func):
    @functools.wraps(func)
    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):
        if func.__name__ == 'variance':
            return func(*args, **kwargs)
        else:
            return func(*args, **kwargs)
    return wrapper

# Example usage:

@myFun
def mean(x):
    return sum(x) / len(x)

@myFun
def variance(x):
    return sum((xi - sum(x)) ** 2 for xi in x) / (len(x) - 1)

print(mean([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))  # Output: 3.0
print(variance([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))  # Output: 2.0
```

Questions:
Title: Cypress - Comparing element text within an array loop.
Body: <p>I have a page with card elements, and more cards are loaded as the user scrolls down. I am trying to write a test to scroll down until all the card elements are loaded.</p>
<p>What I am trying:</p>
<ol>
<li>Get the text of the last card on the page (lastCard)</li>
<li>Scroll down</li>
<li>Get the text of the last card that just loaded (currentCard)</li>
<li>If both cards have the same text, then end the loop. Repeat if not.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>let stopScroll
let currentCard
let lastCard
let scrollArray = Array.from({length:100},(v,k)=&gt;k+1)
    cy.wrap(scrollArray).each((index) =&gt; {
            if (stopScroll != true) {
                cy.get('[data-testid=&quot;card&quot;]').last().then(($element) =&gt; {
                    lastCard = $element.text();
                })
                cy.get('[data-testid=&quot;footer&quot;]').scrollIntoView().should('be.visible')
                cy.wait(1000)
                cy.get('[data-testid=&quot;card&quot;]').last().then(($element) =&gt; {
                    currentCard = $element.text();
                    if (lastCard == currentCard){
                        stopScroll = true
                        cy.log(stopScroll)
                        }
                })
                .then(() =&gt; {
                    if (stopScroll == true){
                    return false
                }
              })
            }
        })
</code></pre>
<p>The loop keeps going on even though stopScroll is being set to true.
Everything seems to work up to the point of &quot;.then(()&quot;. I am unsure whether this is the correct way to go about this.</p>

Title: extern variable prototype: comparison of array not equal to a null pointer is always true.
Body: <p>while cleaning an old program GCC returned a warning that I could not understand.
Here is a snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

extern int *foo[];

int main(void) {
    if (foo != NULL)
        printf(&quot;Foo is not null\n&quot;);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ gcc -Wall main.c
main.c: In function 'main':
main.c:7:17: warning: the comparison will always evaluate as 'true' for the address of 'foo' will never be NULL [-Waddress]
    7 |         if (foo != NULL)
      |                 ^~
main.c:4:13: note: 'foo' declared here
    4 | extern int *foo[];
      |      
</code></pre>
<p>I then tried to change the prototype to <code>extern int **foo;</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$ gcc -Wall main.c
/usr/bin/ld: /tmp/cciux1Df.o: warning: relocation against `foo' in read-only section `.text'
</code></pre>
<ol>
<li><p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/1790720/10645806">As demonstrated here</a>, without initialisation they should be considered the same.
Why <code>**foo</code> (rightfully) fails to compile as an undefined reference, while GCC successfully compiles with <code>*foo[]</code>?</p>
</li>
<li><p>What does the error <code>relocation against 'foo' in read-only section '.text'</code> really means as a warning?</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Thanks.</p>

Answers:
extern int **foo;  // extern variable prototype: comparison of array not equal to a null pointer is always true.

The issue is due to the way the compiler handles arrays and pointers in C. When you declare an array, it allocates memory for all elements in the array. However, when you declare a pointer to an array, it only points to the first element of the array. 

In your case, `foo` is an array of integers, but you're trying to pass it as a pointer to another array. This is why GCC is warning you about the relocation against `foo` in a read-only section.

To fix this, you should use `*foo` instead of `foo` when passing the array to another function. 

Here's the corrected code:

```c
extern int **foo;

int main() {
    if (*foo != NULL)  // Use *
    {
        printf("Foo is not null\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

