To send a POST request to your calculator service in Postman, you need to follow these steps:

1. Create a new request in Postman.
2. Select the "Post" option under the "Request" menu.
3. In the "Body" section, select "raw" and choose "JSON" from the dropdown menu.
4. In the JSON editor, add the following code to your request body:
```json
{
  "operation": "addition",
  "parameters": {
    "a": {"type": "integer"},
    "b": {"type": "integer"}
  },
  "return": {"type": "integer"}
}
```
This will send a POST request to your calculator service with the `addition` function as the operation and the `a` and `b` parameters as the request body.

Alternatively, you can also use the "Body" section of the request to send a JSON object directly. You can create a Python dictionary to represent the JSON object and send it as the request body:
```python
import json

data = {
    "operation": "addition",
    "parameters": {
        "a": {"type": "integer"},
        "b": {"type": "integer"}
    },
    "return": {"type": "integer"}
}

json_data = json.dumps(data)
```
Send this JSON data as the request body in Postman.

In your Python code, you can modify the `@rpc` decorator to accept the request body as a JSON object:
```python
class CalculatorService(ServiceBase):

    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Integer)
    def addition(ctx, a, b):
        data = {"operation": "addition", "parameters": {"a": {"type": "integer"}, "b": {"type": "integer"}}, "return": {"type": "integer"}}
        return a + b

    @rpc(Integer, Integer, _returns=Integer)
    def subtraction(ctx, a, b):
        data = {"operation": "subtraction", "parameters": {"a": {"type": "integer"}, "b": {"type": "integer"}}, "return": {"type": "integer"}}
        return a - b

    # ... (other methods remain the same)
```
This will allow you to send the request body as a JSON object directly in Postman.

